exports[`rendered output 00-start-here.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-start" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc" data-name="dimm-city-design-guide" data-source-range="2:3"><h1 data-name="dimm-city-design-guide">Dimm City Design Guide</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The Dimm City design system for Gutterpress: how to write a Dimm City book in markdown, and what every macro, component, and page template looks like when it prints. Every example in this guide is live — the markdown shown is rendered by the same package the Field Guide uses, so what you see is what a book gets.</p>
<div class="dg-toc">
<h2 data-name="part-1-using-the-system">Part 1 — Using the system</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#ch-writing">Writing a Page</a> — markers, attributes, the markdown the system styles</li>
<li><a href="#ch-typography">Typography</a> — the three faces, the type scale, heading chrome</li>
<li><a href="#ch-palette">Color</a> — paper, fire and HUD tokens, and how to retheme</li>
<li><a href="#ch-layout">Layout</a> — columns, panels, floated callouts, floats, break control</li>
<li><a href="#ch-text">Text &amp; Callouts</a> — ledes, flavor, pull quotes, the alert family, tape, tags</li>
<li><a href="#ch-panels">Panels, Cards &amp; Data</a> — blocks, definitions, procedures, outcomes and roll tables, cards, gear, factions, tables, stat blocks</li>
<li><a href="#ch-specialty">The Specialty System</a> — specialties, learning paths, skill cards</li>
<li><a href="#ch-templates">Page Templates</a> — chapter openers, contents, credits, catalog and profile pages</li>
<li><a href="#ch-publishing">Publishing</a> — preview, build, validate</li>
<li><a href="#ch-reference">Reference</a> — every marker on one page; options, classes, tokens</li>
<li><a href="#ch-variants">Variant Gallery</a> — every element with more than one variant, side by side: all ten skill trees, skill cards, intros, cards</li>
</ol>
<h2 data-name="part-2-a-book-page-by-page">Part 2 — A book, page by page</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#ch-examples">About the examples</a></li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-front-matter">Front Matter</a> — contents, credits, introduction</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-chapter-opener">Chapter Opener</a> — chapter-start page, fiction excerpt, citizen file walkthrough</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-specialty-overview">Specialty Overview</a> — the specialty card grid</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-specialty-profile">Specialty Profile</a> — intro, art, a learning path with skill cards</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-rules">Rules &amp; Mechanics</a> — sections, definitions, conditions, the outcome ladder</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-dm-npcs">Dream Master Pages</a> — DM notes, NPC stat blocks</li>
<li><a href="#ch-example-gear-tech">Gear &amp; Tech</a> — gear entries, callouts, an ego-point ladder</li>
</ol>
</div>
<hr data-source-range="40:41">
<h2 data-name="how-to-read-this-guide">How to read this guide</h2>
<p>Every component page follows the same shape. The markdown you type is shown in a box labelled <strong>You write</strong>; directly beneath it, labelled <strong>Result</strong>, is that exact markdown rendered by the package. Options, variants, and the tokens you can retune follow in a short table. When a component only makes sense on a whole page, the page is shown in Part 2 and linked from the component.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="46:53" class="language-markdown">@lede

This is what you type.

@end-lede
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-intro" data-source-range="58:59"><p>This is what you type.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Three rules carry through the whole system, and they are worth knowing before anything else:</p>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="68:69"><strong>A marker is a line that starts with <code>@</code>.</strong> <code>@lede</code> opens a block, <code>@end-lede</code> closes it. Leave a blank line on each side of every marker.</li>
<li data-source-range="69:70"><strong>Markers take attributes</strong> — <code>#id</code>, <code>.class</code>, and <code>key=&quot;value&quot;</code> — in any order after the name: <code>@section panel #rules .dc-snug</code>.</li>
<li data-source-range="70:72"><strong>Classes on a parent change everything inside it.</strong> <code>@specialty .augmerc</code> gives every card, path, and intro within it the Augmerc shape and color; nothing is set card by card.</li>
</ol>
<h2 data-name="pick-a-section">Pick a section</h2>
<p>Start from what you are writing, not from a class name: find it on the left and write what is on the right. The same table is in the package’s README.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="76:77">
<tr data-source-range="76:77">
<th>I am writing…</th>
<th>Use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="78:106">
<tr data-source-range="78:79">
<td>Rules or reference text under a heading</td>
<td><code>@section panel</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="79:80">
<td>Unboxed text that carries the accent rail</td>
<td><code>@section flat</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="80:81">
<td>A table or list to look up (prices, conditions, a menu of options)</td>
<td><code>@section reference</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="81:82">
<td>World or setting text</td>
<td><code>@section lore</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="82:83">
<td>Advice for the Dream Master, behind the screen</td>
<td><code>@section dm</code> (a short one: <code>@callout dm</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="83:84">
<td>A choice the player writes on the Citizen File</td>
<td><code>@section choose</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="84:85">
<td>A term and its definition, or a glossary of terms</td>
<td><code>@definition</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="85:86">
<td>A boxed note, warning, DM note, vibe, origin or visit</td>
<td><code>@callout …</code> (<code>@dm-note</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="86:87">
<td>A worked example</td>
<td><code>@callout example</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="87:88">
<td>A box in one of three registers: panel, slate, shard</td>
<td><code>@block …</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="88:89">
<td>Fiction, a chapter-opener story</td>
<td><code>@fiction-excerpt</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="89:90">
<td>A creature or NPC</td>
<td><code>@npc-stat</code> (small ones: <code>@npc-stat compact</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="90:91">
<td>An organization: megacorp, cabal, cult, enforcers</td>
<td><code>@faction megacorp</code>, <code>cabal</code>, <code>cult</code>, <code>enforcer</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="91:92">
<td>General gear</td>
<td><code>@gear</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="92:93">
<td>A cybernetic or mod</td>
<td><code>@gear augment</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="93:94">
<td>A magic item or relic</td>
<td><code>@gear treasure</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="94:95">
<td>A vehicle</td>
<td><code>@gear vehicle</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="95:96">
<td>A drug, elixir or consumable</td>
<td><code>@gear drug</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="96:97">
<td>A labelled line in an entry: SPECS, Traits, Effects…</td>
<td><code>**SPECS:**</code> (or <code>@field specs</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="97:98">
<td>A titled run of entries, two columns</td>
<td><code>@section catalog</code> with <code>@columns 2</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="98:99">
<td>A roll-result ladder</td>
<td>a pipe table of the five bands (20, 11-19, 6-10, 2-5, 1), or <code>@outcome</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="99:100">
<td>A random table with equal bands (1-4, 5-8, …)</td>
<td><code>@roll-table</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="100:101">
<td>Flaw, ideal or dream choices</td>
<td><code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code>, <code>@dreams</code> (each a <code>choose</code> section holding a run of <code>@card</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="101:102">
<td>The Citizen File field list</td>
<td><code>@citizen-walkthrough</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="102:103">
<td>The specialty catalog</td>
<td><code>@card-grid</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="103:104">
<td>A specialty, its learning paths and skill cards</td>
<td><code>@specialty</code>, <code>@learning-path</code>, <code>@skill</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="104:105">
<td>Grouping, with no styling</td>
<td><code>@section</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="105:106">
<td>Where it sits</td>
<td><code>@page chapter-start</code>, <code>intro</code>, <code>credits</code>, <code>contents</code>, <code>citizen-file</code> (and core’s <code>blank</code>, <code>full-bleed</code>); <code>@chapter rules</code>, <code>lore</code>, <code>citizen-file</code>, <code>specialty</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The named components are also words on <code>@section</code> (<code>@npc-stat</code> is <code>@section npc</code>, <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> is <code>@section fiction</code>); either spelling renders the same. <code>reference</code>, <code>lore</code>, <code>dm</code> and <code>choose</code> are words only: panels that say what kind of text they hold. Write the content and the marker that says what it is: the defaults set how it looks and where it breaks (sections split across pages, a creature and a callout stay whole, a catalog never does). A page or chapter word never draws a box of its own: it re-tunes the colour and spacing of the sections inside it. To change how one block sits (keep it on one page, tighten it, float a callout), see the author overrides in <a href="#ch-layout" data-gp-source-token="[Layout](#ch-layout)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Layout</a>. <a href="#ch-writing" data-gp-source-token="[Writing a Page](#ch-writing)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Writing a Page</a> covers the grammar, <a href="#ch-panels" data-gp-source-token="[Panels &amp; Cards](#ch-panels)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Panels &amp; Cards</a> the sections, and <a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a> the page and chapter words.</p>
<h2 data-name="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
<p>Install the package into a book, write, and preview:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="113:118" class="language-sh">gutterpress new &quot;My Book&quot; --preset dtrpg
gutterpress ext add gp-dimm-city my-book
gutterpress preview my-book
</code></pre>
<p>Or, in the Gutterpress app: open the book, then Project settings → Features → <em>Install from npm</em> → <code>gp-dimm-city</code>. The package’s macros appear in the snippet picker.</p>
<p>Working on the package itself? This guide loads it from the repository it lives in (<code>extensions: - ../</code> in <code>manifest.yaml</code>), so <code>npm run dev</code> at the repository root previews every change to <code>plugin.js</code> or a stylesheet here, live. The developer loop is in the repository’s <code>docs/developing.md</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 01-writing-a-page.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-writing" data-ch="1" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-1" data-name="writing-a-page" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 1</p>
<h1 data-name="writing-a-page">Writing a Page</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">A Dimm City book is plain markdown with a small vocabulary of <code>@</code> markers. Markdown carries the text; markers draw the structure — chapters, pages, sections, and the components the rest of this guide catalogs. This chapter is the grammar.</p>
<h2 data-name="the-structure-markers">The structure markers</h2>
<p>Gutterpress itself provides the structural markers. They wrap everything that follows until the matching <code>@end-</code> marker, the next marker of the same kind, or the end of the file.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="14:15">
<tr data-source-range="14:15">
<th>Marker</th>
<th>Closes with</th>
<th>What it is</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="16:23">
<tr data-source-range="16:17">
<td><code>@chapter #id ch=&quot;N&quot;</code></td>
<td>next <code>@chapter</code> / end of file</td>
<td>A chapter. <code>ch=&quot;N&quot;</code> prints the <code>c.N</code> footer on every page of it</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<td><code>@page .class</code></td>
<td>next <code>@page</code></td>
<td>A page. Classes pick a page template (<a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="18:19">
<td><code>@spread</code></td>
<td>next <code>@spread</code> / <code>@page</code></td>
<td>Two facing pages kept together</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td><code>@section [word] [.class]</code></td>
<td><code>@end-section</code></td>
<td>A region on the page. Bare, it is a plain box; <code>@section panel</code> is the card</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td><code>@columns 2</code></td>
<td><code>@end-columns</code></td>
<td>Set what is between in 2 to 5 columns</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="21:22">
<td><code>@page-break</code></td>
<td>—</td>
<td>Force a new page here</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="22:23">
<td><code>@column-break</code></td>
<td>—</td>
<td>Force the next column, inside <code>@columns</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A chapter is one file. A <code>@chapter</code> line opens each file, and the chapter ends where the file ends.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="26:37" class="language-markdown">@chapter #ch-augmerc ch=&quot;3&quot;

@section panel

## The Augmerc

Muscle for hire. The difference is gear, grafts, and how much of them is still original.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-name="the-augmerc" data-source-range="42:43"><h2 data-name="the-augmerc">The Augmerc</h2>
<p>Muscle for hire. The difference is gear, grafts, and how much of them is still original.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>@section panel</code> is the card: cream substrate, accent rail, clipped corner, shadow, and the first <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> as a title bar. A bare <code>@section</code> is core’s plain box: it groups what is inside it and has no look of its own beyond a sheet of paper under the text (text is never set straight on the wall). Between them are <code>@section flat</code> (the rail, no card), and four kinds of text (<code>reference</code>, <code>lore</code>, <code>dm</code>, <code>choose</code>); <a href="#ch-panels" data-gp-source-token="[Panels &amp; Cards](#ch-panels)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Panels &amp; Cards</a> shows them. For columns, wrap the content in <code>@columns 2</code>.</p>
<p>Sections split across pages by default. Add <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> to keep one whole.</p>
<p>The named components have a marker of their own, and each is also a word on <code>@section</code>: <code>@npc-stat</code> is <code>@section npc</code>, <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> is <code>@section fiction</code>, <code>@citizen-walkthrough</code> is <code>@section walkthrough</code>, <code>@card-grid</code> is <code>@section cards</code>, and <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> and <code>@dreams</code> are <code>@section flaws</code>, <code>@section ideals</code> and <code>@section dreams</code>. Either spelling renders the same. Not sure which to use? <a href="#ch-start" data-gp-source-token="[Pick a section](#ch-start)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Pick a section</a> maps what you are writing to the marker.</p>
<h2 data-name="attributes">Attributes</h2>
<p>Every marker takes the same attribute grammar after its name, in any order: <code>#id</code> sets an id, <code>.class</code> adds a class, <code>key=value</code> or <code>key=&quot;quoted value&quot;</code> sets a named option.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="62:67" class="language-markdown">@section panel #core-loop .dc-avoid-split

@callout warning label=&quot;Heat is shared&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Standard markdown elements take attributes too, with braces at the end of the element: <code>## Title {.dc-spray}</code>, <code>![alt](img.png){.dc-img-float-right}</code>, and <code>@skill {.dc-avoid-split}</code> for a class on a component macro.</p>
<h2 data-name="the-blank-line-rule">The blank-line rule</h2>
<p>Put a blank line before and after every marker. Gutterpress reads a marker even when it sits straight under a list item or a line of text, but the blank line keeps the page readable and keeps a marker from being mistaken for prose.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="74:81" class="language-markdown">@callout note

Pick a Spec. Spend 6 Spec Points.

@end-callout
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note" data-callout="note" data-source-range="86:87"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p>Pick a Spec. Spend 6 Spec Points.</p>
</div></div>
<h2 data-name="markdown-the-system-styles">Markdown the system styles</h2>
<p>Nothing below needs a class or a marker. The package styles the standard elements the way a Dimm City page expects.</p>
<h3 data-name="headings">Headings</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="100:106" class="language-markdown"># Chapter title
## Section heading
### Sub-section
#### Item heading
</code></pre>
<p><code>#</code> opens a chapter or a specialty. <code>##</code> is a section with an accent rule. <code>###</code> labels a column or a sub-topic. <code>####</code> is an item: a skill, a gear entry, a stat-block name. In a two-column layout, <code>###</code> and <code>####</code> are the workhorses — <code>#</code> and <code>##</code> are too wide for a 3.5-inch column.</p>
<h3 data-name="emphasis-and-inline-code">Emphasis and inline code</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="111:116" class="language-markdown">**Bold** lands in burnt orange.
*Italic* shifts to warm smoke.
\`SysChk\` marks a keyword that is also a mechanic.
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p><strong>Bold</strong> lands in burnt orange. <em>Italic</em> shifts to warm smoke. <code>SysChk</code> marks a keyword that is also a mechanic.</p>
</div>
<h3 data-name="lists">Lists</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="125:132" class="language-markdown">- A loose list
- of short items

1. An ordered list
2. for sequences
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<ul>
<li data-source-range="135:136">A loose list</li>
<li data-source-range="136:138">of short items</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="138:139">An ordered list</li>
<li data-source-range="139:141">for sequences</li>
</ol>
</div>
<h3 data-name="tables">Tables</h3>
<p>Pipe tables get the header band, alternating fills, and the data type size. Roll tables, option tables, and stat references are all plain tables.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="147:153" class="language-markdown">| Band | Range |
|---|---|
| **Reach** | Adjacent |
| **Near** | Same room |
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<table>
<thead data-source-range="156:157">
<tr data-source-range="156:157">
<th>Band</th>
<th>Range</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="158:160">
<tr data-source-range="158:159">
<td><strong>Reach</strong></td>
<td>Adjacent</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="159:160">
<td><strong>Near</strong></td>
<td>Same room</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3 data-name="blockquote">Blockquote</h3>
<p>A plain <code>&gt;</code> quote is an epigraph: accent rail, italic body. Inside a skill card the first <code>&gt;</code> line is the card’s flavor.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="167:172" class="language-markdown">&gt; Every city has a language.
&gt;
&gt; — Hollis Vance
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<blockquote>
<p data-source-range="175:176">Every city has a language.</p>
<p data-source-range="177:178">— Hollis Vance</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<h3 data-name="definition-list">Definition list</h3>
<p>A term line and a <code>: definition</code> line render as the on-brand glossary form.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="185:192" class="language-markdown">Tick
: A heartbeat. Used in ability text to mean *immediately*.

Reach
: Close enough to touch.
</code></pre>
<div class="dg-stage">
<dl>
<dt data-source-range="195:195">Tick</dt>
<dd data-source-range="196:198">A heartbeat. Used in ability text to mean <em>immediately</em>.</dd>
<dt data-source-range="198:198">Reach</dt>
<dd data-source-range="198:201">Close enough to touch.</dd>
</dl>
</div>
<h3 data-name="smart-typography-and-footnotes">Smart typography and footnotes</h3>
<p><code>--</code> becomes an en dash, <code>---</code> an em dash, <code>...</code> an ellipsis, and straight quotes curl. Footnotes use <code>[^label]</code> inline and <code>[^label]: text</code> anywhere in the file; they render at the end of the content flow.</p>
<h3 data-name="images">Images</h3>
<p>An image on its own line is a figure. Place it in the flow where it should sit; floats and framing are classes, described in <a href="#ch-layout" data-gp-source-token="[Layout](#ch-layout)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Layout</a>.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="211:214" class="language-markdown">![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png)
</code></pre>
<h2 data-name="a-complete-page">A complete page</h2>
<p>Putting the pieces together — the shape of an ordinary body page in a Dimm City book:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="219:242" class="language-markdown">@chapter #ch-rules ch=&quot;4&quot;

@section panel

## Augment Points

@columns 2

**AP** is your push. If an ability lists an AP cost, you spend it and the effect happens.

@column-break

- **0 AP** — Free to use. Not always safe.
- **1–X AP** — You choose how hard you push.

@end-columns

@end-section

&gt; [!NOTE]
&gt; You start every dream with 10 AP.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-name="augment-points" data-source-range="247:248"><h2 data-name="augment-points">Augment Points</h2>
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="251:252"><p><strong>AP</strong> is your push. If an ability lists an AP cost, you spend it and the effect happens.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="255:256"></div>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="257:258"><strong>0 AP</strong> — Free to use. Not always safe.</li>
<li data-source-range="258:260"><strong>1–X AP</strong> — You choose how hard you push.</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div class="dc-alert dc-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p data-source-range="264:266">You start every dream with 10 AP.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 02-typography.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter typography dg-guide" id="ch-typography" data-ch="2" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-2" data-name="typography" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 2</p>
<h1 data-name="typography">Typography</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">Three faces, each with one job. lixdu is the display face for chapter and section headings and banners; Tomorrow is the mono face for tabs, chips, counters, and code; Titillium Web carries every line of body, flavor, and quote copy.</p>
<h2 data-name="the-type-scale">The type scale</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="12:13">
<tr data-source-range="12:13">
<th>Element</th>
<th>You write</th>
<th>Size · Face</th>
<th>Role</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="14:24">
<tr data-source-range="14:15">
<td>H1</td>
<td><code># Title</code></td>
<td>26pt bold · lixdu</td>
<td>Chapter and specialty openers</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="15:16">
<td>H2</td>
<td><code>## Heading</code></td>
<td>20pt bold · lixdu</td>
<td>Section breaks, with an accent rule</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="16:17">
<td>H3</td>
<td><code>### Sub-heading</code></td>
<td>16pt · lixdu</td>
<td>Column labels and sub-topics</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<td>H4</td>
<td><code>#### Item</code></td>
<td>13pt uppercase · lixdu</td>
<td>Skill names, gear names, stat-block names</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="18:19">
<td>Body</td>
<td>a paragraph</td>
<td>12pt · Titillium Web</td>
<td>All prose</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td>Flavor</td>
<td><code>&gt; [!FLAVOR]</code></td>
<td>11pt italic · Titillium Web</td>
<td>In-world voice</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td>Chevron banner</td>
<td><code># Title {.dc-chevron}</code></td>
<td>H1 scale, angled fill</td>
<td>One per chapter opener</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="21:22">
<td>Spray banner</td>
<td><code>## Title {.dc-spray}</code></td>
<td>H2 scale, a stencilled tag with a stepped edge</td>
<td>Learning paths, major topic breaks</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="22:23">
<td>Card tab</td>
<td>emitted by <code>@skill</code></td>
<td>11pt title, 10pt tier pill · Tomorrow</td>
<td>Skill-card tab labels</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="23:24">
<td>Tag</td>
<td><code>&lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;</code></td>
<td>10pt · Tomorrow</td>
<td>Keyword and cost chips</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 data-name="the-three-faces">The three faces</h2>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>lixdu</strong> — display · <code>--font-display</code> · headings set uppercase, H1 26pt, leading 1.35, tracking 0.2em</p>
<p class="dg-face-display">ABCDEFGHIJKLM<br>NOPQRSTUVWXYZ<br>0123456789</p>
<p class="dg-face-display dg-face-set">Wired to kill</p>
</div>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>Titillium Web</strong> — body · <code>--font-body</code> · 12pt, leading 1.5 (18pt), tracking 0.005em</p>
<p class="dg-face-body">ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz 0123456789 &amp;?!</p>
<p class="dg-face-body dg-face-set">Corporate enforcers earn their grafts in blood and overtime; street muscle runs cheaper and lasts longer than anyone admits. <em>Italic carries the in-world voice,</em> and <strong>bold marks a rule.</strong></p>
</div>
<div class="dg-face">
<p class="dg-face-meta"><strong>Tomorrow</strong> — labels · <code>--font-mono</code>, <code>--font-tab</code> · tabs, chips and counters at 10pt (the floor), uppercase, tracking 0.08em or wider</p>
<p class="dg-face-tab">ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz 0123456789</p>
<p class="dg-face-tab dg-face-set">AUG1.3 · 2 AP · SYSCHK 9–13</p>
</div>
<h2 data-name="heading-hierarchy">Heading hierarchy</h2>
<p>Each level rendered at its print size:</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<h1 data-name="chapter-title">Chapter Title</h1>
<h2 data-name="section-heading">Section Heading</h2>
<h3 data-name="sub-section-label">Sub-section Label</h3>
<h4 data-name="item-heading">Item Heading</h4>
</div>
<h2 data-name="level-and-look">Level and look</h2>
<p>A heading’s level is its place in the outline, and the outline never skips a level: <code>#</code> is followed by <code>##</code>, never <code>###</code>. Gutterpress flags a skip when it builds. When the page needs a smaller label than the outline allows, keep the level and set the look with <code>.dc-h3</code>, <code>.dc-h4</code>, <code>.dc-h5</code> or <code>.dc-h6</code>:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="65:74" class="language-markdown"># Cybernetics, Weapons, and Gear

## Useful Items {.dc-h3}

### Base Reality {.dc-h4}

#### Dreamer {.dc-h5}
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<h2 class="dc-h3" data-name="useful-items">Useful Items</h2>
<h3 class="dc-h4" data-name="base-reality">Base Reality</h3>
<h4 class="dc-h5" data-name="dreamer">Dreamer</h4>
</div>
<p>The look follows the class everywhere: an <code>##</code> with <code>.dc-h3</code> that opens a section takes the subordinate section bar, and a <code>###</code> with <code>.dc-h4</code> inside a section takes no accent rule. On a paragraph, the class sets a line in heading type without adding it to the outline.</p>
<h2 data-name="heading-chrome">Heading chrome</h2>
<p>Three classes dress a heading. The chevron opens a chapter, the spray breaks a major topic, and the spec-tweak rule flags an optional mechanic; <code>.dc-no-top</code> pulls it tight against whatever is above it.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="93:100" class="language-markdown"># Augmerc {.dc-chevron}

## Biting Distance {.dc-spray}

### Spec Tweak: Wired to Kill {.dc-spec-tweak .dc-no-top}
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-name="augmerc" data-source-range="105:106"><h1 class="dc-chevron" data-name="augmerc">Augmerc</h1>
<h2 class="dc-spray" data-name="biting-distance">Biting Distance</h2>
<h3 class="dc-spec-tweak dc-no-top" data-name="spec-tweak-wired-to-kill">Spec Tweak: Wired to Kill</h3>
</div></div>
<h2 data-name="body-and-flavor">Body and flavor</h2>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>Twelve-point Titillium Web carries all running narrative. Corporate enforcers earn their grafts in blood and overtime; street muscle runs cheaper and lasts longer than anyone admits. Notice the leading, the x-height, and how weight shifts when a word is <strong>bolded</strong> or <em>italicized</em> mid-sentence.</p>
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="123:125">See an opening, ya take it. Best time to hit 'em is when they think it's over.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Flavor is 11pt italic in <code>--ink-dark</code>, with a rail and no box. Never set flavor in bold italic — it reads as urgency, not voice.</p>
<p class="dg-dont-label">Don’t</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-dont">
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="134:136"><em><strong>See an opening, ya take it. Best time to hit 'em is when they think it's over.</strong></em></p>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Column-safe headings:</strong> in a two-column layout use <code>###</code> and <code>####</code>. <code>#</code> and <code>##</code> at full print size exceed a 3.5-inch column.</p>
<h2 data-name="size-floors">Size floors</h2>
<p>There is one floor, <code>--fs-floor</code> (10pt), and nothing prints smaller: not a chip, a tab, a tier pill, an AP chip, a folio or a stamp. It is a minimum, not a target; text that already meets it stays as it is.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="145:146">
<tr data-source-range="145:146">
<th>Text</th>
<th>Floor</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="147:148">
<tr data-source-range="147:148">
<td>All text: body, rules, tables, captions, labels, chips, tabs and badges</td>
<td>10pt (the book sets running body at 12pt)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 data-name="smart-typography">Smart typography</h2>
<p>The renderer converts ASCII shortcuts as you type: <code>--</code> is an en dash --, <code>---</code> an em dash —, <code>...</code> an ellipsis …, and “straight quotes” curl. No special syntax, no Unicode entry.</p>
<h2 data-name="font-tokens">Font tokens</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="155:156">
<tr data-source-range="155:156">
<th>Token</th>
<th>Face</th>
<th>Used for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="157:162">
<tr data-source-range="157:158">
<td><code>--font-display</code></td>
<td>lixdu</td>
<td>H1–H3, banners</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="158:159">
<td><code>--font-body</code></td>
<td>Titillium Web</td>
<td>Body prose, flavor, quotes</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="159:160">
<td><code>--font-mono</code></td>
<td>Tomorrow</td>
<td>Code, counters, tabular data</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="160:161">
<td><code>--font-tab</code></td>
<td>Tomorrow</td>
<td>Short label text: card tabs, stat labels</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="161:162">
<td><code>--font-quote</code></td>
<td>Titillium Web</td>
<td>Italic quotes and attributions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The fonts ship in the package and embed in the PDF at build time; a book never installs them.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 03-palette.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter palette dg-guide" id="ch-palette" data-ch="3" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-3" data-name="color" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 3</p>
<h1 data-name="color">Color</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">Cream paper, dark ink, and two accent registers. Creaturepunk <strong>fire</strong> — crimson, orange, rust, amber — for printed lore, ability text, and danger. <strong>HUD digital</strong> — blue and cyan — for rules, look-ups, cybernetics and system chrome, with magenta kept for display punch. The page is never black, and fire and HUD never share one element.</p>
<h2 data-name="paper-ink">Paper &amp; ink</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>BG</strong><code>#c8c5bf</code><code>--bg</code><code>21/18/22/0<br>61%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Cream</strong><code>#f0eee9</code><code>--paper-cream</code><code>4/4/7/0<br>14%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Light</strong><code>#e2ded7</code><code>--paper-light</code><code>10/9/13/0<br>32%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch border on-light"><strong>Paper Aged</strong><code>#c8c2b8</code><code>--paper-aged</code><code>21/20/26/0<br>66%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink on-dark"><strong>Ink</strong><code>#1e1d1c</code><code>--ink</code><code>69/66/67/76<br>278%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink-dark on-dark"><strong>Ink Dark</strong><code>#2f2622</code><code>--ink-dark</code><code>61/66/69/69<br>266%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink-smoke on-dark"><strong>Ink Smoke</strong><code>#4d4339</code><code>--ink-smoke</code><code>57/60/68/47<br>231%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-ink-dust on-dark"><strong>Ink Dust</strong><code>#665b4e</code><code>--ink-dust</code><code>53/54/64/29<br>200%</code></div>
</div>
<p>The page background (<code>--bg</code>) is the wall; a brick texture is painted over it by the package’s <code>@page</code> rule. Paper surfaces are what sits on the wall — cards, callouts, panels. Components are posters and displays hung on the wall: hard edges, decisive separation, never a soft tint that lets the texture through.</p>
<h2 data-name="creaturepunk-fire">Creaturepunk fire</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-crimson on-dark"><strong>Crimson</strong><code>#e1261c</code><code>--crimson</code><code>5/98/100/0<br>203%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-blood on-dark"><strong>Blood</strong><code>#901a12</code><code>--blood</code><code>27/99/100/28<br>254%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-orange on-dark"><strong>Orange</strong><code>#d4500a</code><code>--orange</code><code>11/82/100/2<br>195%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-rust on-dark"><strong>Rust</strong><code>#b23a12</code><code>--rust</code><code>21/89/100/12<br>222%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-amber on-light"><strong>Amber</strong><code>#7a5a20</code><code>--amber</code><code>41/57/100/29<br>227%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-deep-rust on-dark"><strong>Deep Rust</strong><code>#70200e</code><code>--deep-rust</code><code>32/91/100/45<br>268%</code></div>
</div>
<h2 data-name="hud-digital">HUD digital</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue on-dark"><strong>HUD Blue</strong><code>#1f6f94</code><code>--hud-blue</code><code>87/50/26/4<br>167%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue-bright on-light"><strong>HUD Bright</strong><code>#00bcd4</code><code>--hud-blue-bright</code><code>70/1/16/0<br>87%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-blue-dim border on-light"><strong>HUD Dim</strong><code>#7ab8d0</code><code>--hud-blue-dim</code><code>50/13/12/0<br>76%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-magenta on-dark"><strong>HUD Magenta</strong><code>#c026d3</code><code>--hud-magenta</code><code>41/85/0/0<br>126%</code></div>
</div>
<h2 data-name="surfaces-borders">Surfaces &amp; borders</h2>
<div class="dc-palette-grid">
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-hud-panel border on-light"><strong>HUD Panel</strong><code>#eeece8</code><code>--hud-panel</code><code>5/5/7/0<br>16%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-surface-tint-3 border on-light"><strong>Surface 3</strong><code>#f2f0ec</code><code>--surface-tint-3</code><code>3/4/6/0<br>12%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-surface-orange-tint border on-light"><strong>Orange Tint</strong><code>#d8cfb4</code><code>--surface-orange-tint</code><code>14/14/30/0<br>59%</code></div>
  <div class="dc-palette-swatch bg-border-hairline on-light"><strong>Hairline</strong><code>#c8b898</code><code>--border-hairline</code><code>22/24/42/0<br>88%</code></div>
</div>
<p><code>--paper-stain</code> (<code>#b0a89c</code>) is a dark inset well and label strip: it never carries body text, and neither does any fill darker than the wall. <code>--paper-form</code> (<code>#f5f3ee</code>, a hair lighter than cream: the <code>choose</code> form’s printed sheet) and <code>--paper-zine</code> (<code>#e9e6e0</code>, a printed-page neutral used by the fiction excerpt and the shard block) sit with the paper swatches above. <code>--border-hairline</code> is a rule line. Other rules use the ink scale directly.</p>
<h2 data-name="what-each-colour-means">What each colour means</h2>
<p>Colour is a job, not a mood. A reader flipping pages should know what kind of content a box holds from its fill and silhouette before reading a word, and the same has to hold in a grayscale proof, so every variant also differs in value, edge or label: never in hue alone.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="61:62">
<tr data-source-range="61:62">
<th>Colour</th>
<th>Means</th>
<th>Used by</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="63:71">
<tr data-source-range="63:64">
<td>Magenta</td>
<td>Display punch: the loudest thing on the page, never a category</td>
<td>Chapter chevrons, spray titles, pull quotes, <code>ROLL THE DIE!</code>, the VAR chip, the default panel accent</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="64:65">
<td>HUD blue, pale cyan</td>
<td>A rule or a look-up, for the player</td>
<td>Note alert, reference section, definitions, megacorp faction, ideals</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="65:66">
<td>Cyan on ink</td>
<td>The Dream Master only</td>
<td><code>dm</code> alert and section, the Dream Master chip</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="66:67">
<td>Hazard yellow with ink</td>
<td>Danger, consequence, a choice you must make</td>
<td>Warning alert, enforcer faction, the <em>Choose</em> stamp, vehicle gear</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="67:68">
<td>Blood, rust, crimson</td>
<td>Harm, cost, cult, a binding ruling</td>
<td>Slate block, flaws, cult faction, origin stamp, side effects</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="68:69">
<td>Violet, amethyst</td>
<td>The world and the arcane</td>
<td>Vibe alert, lore section, dreams, cabal faction, treasure</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="69:70">
<td>Fungi green</td>
<td>The living city: organic, toxic</td>
<td>Visit alert, drug gear, Gutterdruid</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="70:71">
<td>Paper versus ink</td>
<td>Who it is for</td>
<td>Light paper is for players, an ink-dark fill is for the Dream Master, aged paper is fiction and the world</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 data-name="usage-rules">Usage rules</h2>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="74:75">Crimson is the dominant accent — one crimson element per composition. Stack further emphasis with orange.</li>
<li data-source-range="75:76">HUD blue means a rule or a look-up; magenta is display punch, not a category. Never mix fire and HUD on the same element.</li>
<li data-source-range="76:77">Paper is for raised elements. The wall is the canvas; cream is the surface.</li>
<li data-source-range="77:79">Keep CMYK coverage under 280% on coated stock and 240% on uncoated. The fire palette runs hot — proof on paper before a print run. The CMYK and percentage printed on each swatch above is the measured coverage in a <code>pdfx</code> build (Ghostscript with the CGATS21_CRPC1 profile), not an estimate.</li>
</ul>
<p>Running text goes on a paper surface, never straight on the wall: the brick texture under body type cuts its contrast and breaks up the letterforms.</p>
<p class="dg-dont-label">Don’t</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-dont">
<p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence. Nothing here is safe, and nothing here is free.</p>
</div>
<p class="dg-result">Do</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-source-range="93:94"><p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence. Nothing here is safe, and nothing here is free.</p>
</div></div>
<h2 data-name="retheming-a-book">Retheming a book</h2>
<p>Every component exposes its colors and shapes as <code>--dc-*</code> custom properties, with defaults in the package’s <code>styles/dc-component-defaults.css</code>. A book never edits the package: it lists one sheet of its own under <code>styles:</code> and resets tokens there, either for the whole book at <code>:root</code> or for one chapter at the chapter’s id. The book’s sheet is unlayered and the package sits in its own cascade layer, so the override wins at any specificity.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="106:117" class="language-css">/* my-book/styles/overrides.css */
:root {
  --dc-flavor-accent: var(--hud-blue);        /* every flavor rail, whole book */
}

#ch-gear {
  --dc-section-accent: var(--amber);          /* section chrome in one chapter */
  --dc-alert-border: var(--rust);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The brand tokens themselves — <code>--crimson</code>, <code>--hud-blue</code>, <code>--paper-cream</code>, the fonts — live in <code>styles/dc-palette.css</code> and reset the same way. The full token-to-component map is in <a href="#ch-reference" data-gp-source-token="[Reference](#ch-reference)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Reference</a>.</p>
<p>Do not write bare <code>.dc-*</code> rules in a book to change a component; that fights the package on every update. Reset the component’s tokens on a context selector instead.</p>
<h2 data-name="cmyk">CMYK</h2>
<p>Each swatch’s third line is its CMYK build (C/M/Y/K, percent), converted from the hex through CGATS21_CRPC1 — the coated press profile gutterpress embeds in a PDF/X build — with relative colorimetric intent. Treat them as starting points: RGB to CMYK is not one-to-one for saturated colors, so request a physical proof before the full print run.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 04-layout.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter layout dg-guide" id="ch-layout" data-ch="4" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-4" data-name="layout" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 4</p>
<h1 data-name="layout">Layout</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">How content is arranged inside a page: columns, floated callouts, floated art, and the handful of classes that control where things break. Everything here is a marker, a class on <code>@section</code>, <code>@page</code>, or an image.</p>
<h2 data-name="columns">Columns</h2>
<p><code>@columns</code> is Gutterpress’s one way to set content in columns: <code>@columns 2</code> to <code>@columns 5</code>, closed by <code>@end-columns</code>. Text fills the first column and overflows into the next; <code>@column-break</code> forces the next column.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="15:26" class="language-markdown">@columns 2

Left column. Text fills top to bottom and overflows right.

@column-break

Right column.

@end-columns
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="31:32"><p>Left column. Text fills top to bottom and overflows right.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="35:36"></div>
<p>Right column.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>@columns 3</code> is the same marker with a 3. A run’s columns balance, so a short run ends level. Whatever wants columns goes inside <code>@columns</code>: paragraphs, a list, a run of entries. The components in this guide take it the same way, for example <code>@columns 2</code> inside a <code>@skill</code>, a <code>@card-grid</code> or an <code>@npc-stat</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="a-card-with-columns">A card with columns</h2>
<p>A column run inside a <code>@section panel</code> sits in the standard card: the leading <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> is the heading bar at full width, and <code>@columns 2</code> below it splits the body.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="49:66" class="language-markdown">@section panel

## The City

@columns 2

Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence.

@column-break

Nothing here is safe. Nothing here is free.

@end-columns

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-name="the-city" data-source-range="71:72"><h2 data-name="the-city">The City</h2>
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="75:76"><p>Dimm City twitches like a clamped nerve at the edge of existence.</p>
<div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="79:80"></div>
<p>Nothing here is safe. Nothing here is free.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h2 data-name="sidebar">Sidebar</h2>
<p>There is no sidebar marker: <code>.dc-sidebar</code> on a callout floats it to the right at about a third of the width, and body text wraps on its left. Give it running prose on both sides so it never strands in white space.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="93:104" class="language-markdown">When the Dreamers push past Too Far, the table starts asking how movement works.

@callout note .dc-sidebar label=&quot;Distance, Fast&quot;

Three bands, no grid. **In Reach** is one swing away. **Nearby** is a burned Move. **Too Far** might as well be the other side of the ether.

@end-callout

Keep the bands fictional, not metric. The Dream Master sets the band; the dice decide whether you close it.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>When the Dreamers push past Too Far, the table starts asking how movement works.</p>
<div class="dc-alert dc-note dc-sidebar" data-callout="note" data-label="Distance, Fast" data-name="distance-fast" data-source-range="111:112"><span class="dc-alert-label">Distance, Fast</span>
<p>Three bands, no grid. <strong>In Reach</strong> is one swing away. <strong>Nearby</strong> is a burned Move. <strong>Too Far</strong> might as well be the other side of the ether.</p>
</div><p>Keep the bands fictional, not metric. The Dream Master sets the band; the dice decide whether you close it. A Dreamer who wants to cross two bands in one turn is telling you they’re willing to spend everything to get there — let them, and let it cost.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="page dg-doc chapter-4" data-name="floated-images" data-source-range="121:122"><h2 data-name="floated-images">Floated images</h2>
<p>An image on its own line is a figure. <code>.dc-img-float-right</code> or <code>.dc-img-float-left</code> floats it at 44% of the column with text wrapping around it; leave a blank line after the paragraph that should clear it.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="127:132" class="language-markdown">![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png){.dc-img-float-right}

Blasts lit the dusk like glitchfire. Bolts and teeth and claws tangled mid-air.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p class="dc-img-wrapper"><img src="img/scavenger.png" alt="Scavenger" class="dc-img-float-right" data-gp-source-token="![Scavenger](img/scavenger.png)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0" data-name="scavenger"></p>
<p>Blasts lit the dusk like glitchfire. Bolts and teeth and claws tangled mid-air. Neon signs cracked. Alleyways bled smoke. Debris rained in bursts. This wasn’t about glory — it was turf. It was pride. It was blood memory, raw and ugly, of family torn away by their rival.</p>
<p>DimmCitz scattered, vanished into bolted dens and reinforced rooftops. The air stank of scorched fur, ozone, and cordite.</p>
</div>
<p>A <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> places its first image for you — see <a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="break-control">Break control</h2>
<p>Callouts keep themselves whole by default: they move to the next page rather than split. A creature (an <code>@npc-stat</code> with at most one <code>####</code>) stays whole too. Sections, factions and skill cards do the opposite: they split where the page ends, at a sensible place (a skill card never opens with only its tab, and an ability row is never cut), and so does a catalog. Two classes override that for one box, and a third forces a break. None is needed to make ordinary content break well.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="151:152">
<tr data-source-range="151:152">
<th>You write</th>
<th>Effect</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="153:158">
<tr data-source-range="153:154">
<td><code>@section panel .dc-avoid-split</code></td>
<td>Keep this section whole: it moves to the next page rather than splitting</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="154:155">
<td><code>@section .gp-no-break</code></td>
<td>Core’s own never-split class, for a section of any kind</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="155:156">
<td><code>@skill {.dc-avoid-split}</code></td>
<td>Keep every card of this skill whole: it moves to the next page rather than splitting</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="156:157">
<td><code>## Heading {.gp-break-before}</code></td>
<td>Start a new page before this element</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="157:158">
<td><code>@page-break</code></td>
<td>Start a new page here</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Reach for <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> on a short section that would read badly in two halves; a section kept whole leaves a dead zone at the foot of the page before it. On a skill card the same class costs more: a card is a third to half a page, so keeping one whole leaves a page mostly empty. Prefer splitting content at a logical boundary first — <code>@continue</code> for a long skill card.</p>
<p>The class is a setter of one token per component, which a book can set from its own sheet instead: <code>--dc-section-break-inside</code>, <code>--dc-skill-card-break-inside</code>, <code>--dc-faction-break-inside</code> and <code>--dc-npc-stat-break-inside</code> (<code>avoid</code> keeps the box whole, <code>auto</code> lets it split). A section kept whole also keeps its padding inside its box, so a book writes <code>--dc-section-edge: 0px</code> beside the first. A catalog splits by default and reads <code>--dc-catalog-break-inside</code>: <code>.dc-avoid-split</code>, or that token set to <code>avoid</code>, keeps one whole.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="163:166" class="language-css">#core-loop { --dc-section-break-inside: avoid; --dc-section-edge: 0px; }
</code></pre>
<h2 data-name="tighter-spacing">Tighter spacing</h2>
<p><code>.dc-snug</code> halves the space between paragraphs and list items inside a section or page. Reach for it when a section overflows by a line or two and the last line would otherwise strand on a page of its own. Type size and leading are untouched.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="171:180" class="language-markdown">@section panel .dc-snug
### Ideal

Paragraph and list-item gaps shrink to 0.04in here.
@end-section

@page .dc-snug
</code></pre>
<p>The class sets <code>--dc-flow-gap</code>, the space under every paragraph and list item in the box: a book sets it on any box from its own sheet (<code>#core-loop { --dc-flow-gap: 0.04in; }</code>). Unset, each component keeps its own spacing.</p>
<h2 data-name="edge-to-edge">Edge to edge</h2>
<p><code>.dc-flush</code> removes a component’s side margins so it runs the full column. It is read by tape, stat blocks, sticker chains, and <code>@outcome flush</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 05-text-and-callouts.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-text" data-ch="5" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-5" data-name="text-callouts" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 5</p>
<h1 data-name="text-callouts">Text &amp; Callouts</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The components that live inside running prose: the lede that opens a chapter, flavor and pull quotes for in-world voice, the alert family for notes a reader must not miss, and the tape, tags, and roll chip that punctuate a page.</p>
<h2 data-name="lede">Lede</h2>
<p>The opening paragraph of a chapter or major section, set larger on its own light panel: upright semibold ink type, a heavy accent rail and an ink foot rule.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="15:22" class="language-markdown">@lede

The city didn't go quiet — it got loud. You came to Dimm City to disappear, and instead the whole block learned your name.

@end-lede
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-intro" data-source-range="27:28"><p>The city didn’t go quiet — it got loud. You came to Dimm City to disappear, and instead the whole block learned your name.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-intro-bg</code>, <code>--dc-intro-accent</code>, <code>--dc-intro-color</code>, <code>--dc-intro-size</code>, <code>--dc-intro-weight</code>, <code>--dc-intro-style</code>, <code>--dc-intro-rail</code>, <code>--dc-intro-rule</code>, <code>--dc-intro-ink</code> (the lede’s text-safe accent ink).</p>
<h2 data-name="flavor">Flavor</h2>
<p>In-world voice: italic, accent-railed, spoken from inside the fiction. It has no box: the rail is the whole container. Inside a <code>@skill</code> card the first <code>&gt;</code> line is flavor automatically; anywhere else, tag the quote.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="41:45" class="language-markdown">&gt; [!FLAVOR]
&gt; Down here we don't ask what you were before the grafts. We ask what's left.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-flavor">
<p data-source-range="50:52">Down here we don't ask what you were before the grafts. We ask what's left.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-flavor-accent</code>, <code>--dc-flavor-accent-width</code>, <code>--dc-flavor-color</code>, <code>--dc-flavor-bg</code> (a flavor line sits on paper, never on the wall).</p>
<h2 data-name="pull-quote">Pull quote</h2>
<p>A large-format excerpt with rules above and below. One per chapter, to let a single line breathe. The attribution goes after a blank <code>&gt;</code> line.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="61:67" class="language-markdown">&gt; [!PULLQUOTE]
&gt; The rig braces and answers every swing.
&gt;
&gt; Field manual, second draft
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-pullquote dc-flush">
<p data-source-range="72:74">The rig braces and answers every swing.</p>
<p data-source-range="75:76">Field manual, second draft</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-pullquote-accent</code>, <code>--dc-pullquote-accent-soft</code>, <code>--dc-pullquote-bg</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="alerts">Alerts</h2>
<p>One shell, eight registers (note, warning, dm, vibe, origin, location, visit, example), told apart by silhouette, fill and label, so a reader knows the kind before reading a word, in colour and in grayscale. Write a GFM alert — a blockquote whose first line is <code>[!TYPE]</code>, for the first seven — and the label, colour, edge and shape come with the type. Use the blockquote form for a single paragraph; for several paragraphs, a list, or a custom label, use the <code>@callout</code> block below.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="86:109">&gt; [!NOTE]
&gt; A character may hold no more than one Signature Augment at a time.

&gt; [!WARNING]
&gt; Trauma Patches stabilize a dying character but do not restore HP.

&gt; [!DM]
&gt; If a player hasn't chosen starting gear by the end of session zero, hand them a Scavenger Pack and move on.

&gt; [!VIBE]
&gt; The rain in Dimm City never quite stops — it just changes color under the signage.

&gt; [!ORIGIN]
&gt; You didn't choose the street — the street chose you.

&gt; [!LOCATION]
&gt; The Undercroft: three levels of flooded parking under the old ArkD tower, lit by whatever the squatters can wire.

&gt; [!VISIT]
&gt; The Neon Bazaar doesn't close. If you can name it, someone here is selling a knockoff of it two stalls down.

</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-tall">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Note</span>
<p data-source-range="114:116">A character may hold no more than one Signature Augment at a time.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-note warning"><span class="dc-alert-label">Warning</span>
<p data-source-range="117:119">Trauma Patches stabilize a dying character but do not restore HP.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-dm-note"><span class="dc-alert-label">Dream Master Note</span>
<p data-source-range="120:122">If a player hasn't chosen starting gear by the end of session zero, hand them a Scavenger Pack and move on.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-vibe-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Vibe</span>
<p data-source-range="123:125">The rain in Dimm City never quite stops — it just changes color under the signage.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-origin-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Origin</span>
<p data-source-range="126:128">You didn't choose the street — the street chose you.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-location-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Location</span>
<p data-source-range="129:131">The Undercroft: three levels of flooded parking under the old ArkD tower, lit by whatever the squatters can wire.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-alert dc-visit-callout"><span class="dc-alert-label">Visit</span>
<p data-source-range="132:134">The Neon Bazaar doesn't close. If you can name it, someone here is selling a knockoff of it two stalls down.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3 data-name="which-alert">Which alert</h3>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="140:141">
<tr data-source-range="140:141">
<th>Type</th>
<th>Who it is for</th>
<th>Register</th>
<th>Looks like</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="142:150">
<tr data-source-range="142:143">
<td><code>NOTE</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A rules clarification</td>
<td>A calm HUD-blue panel with a blue rail and small, even machined chamfers</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="143:144">
<td><code>WARNING</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A rule with consequences</td>
<td>Hazard: an octagonal sign plate, a light cream sheet, a yellow-and-black stripe rail, a heavy ink frame (with a crimson second pass a hair out of register) and a yellow caution-tape label</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="144:145">
<td><code>DM</code></td>
<td>The Dream Master only</td>
<td>Guidance, hooks, adjudication</td>
<td>An ink file folder: a tab rising from the top edge, one corner cut, a cyan rail and a cyan chip at the top right</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="145:146">
<td><code>VIBE</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>Atmosphere and narrative voice, full width</td>
<td>A barely violet sheet with long shallow diagonal corners, one violet edge all round, a strip of tape on top, italic, with a violet-edged label chip</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="146:147">
<td><code>ORIGIN</code></td>
<td>Players, in second person</td>
<td>Backstory addressed to the character</td>
<td>Warm paper with a torn bottom edge, a dog-eared corner, a rust rail and a worn crimson stamp label</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="147:148">
<td><code>LOCATION</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A place written up: what it is, who holds it, how to get in</td>
<td>Origin’s document in the map’s teal: cool map paper with a torn bottom edge, a dog-eared corner, a teal rail and a worn deep-teal stamp label</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="148:149">
<td><code>VISIT</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A location, present tense, before an encounter</td>
<td>A fungi-green signpost with a wobbly, organic outline and a map-pin label</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="149:150">
<td><code>@callout example</code></td>
<td>Players</td>
<td>A worked example (a block only: there is no <code>[!EXAMPLE]</code>)</td>
<td>A ticket (a V notch in its right edge), a cyan rail and a taped-on label</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Every cut edge is padded clear of the text, and every variant sets <code>--dc-accent-ink</code>, the text-safe ink of its own accent, so a <code>ROLL THE DIE!</code> inside takes the box’s colour. Every body is italic except the example’s, which is upright (the two reversed bodies, DM and visit, are semibold) and every label is 10pt mono caps. A chapter can re-theme a panel’s accent, but it never repaints an alert: the register is the same on every page.</p>
<p>Tokens shared by the family: <code>--dc-alert-bg</code>, <code>--dc-alert-border</code>, <code>--dc-alert-fg</code>, <code>--dc-alert-label-color</code>, <code>--dc-alert-border-width</code>, and, for the frame and label chrome, <code>--dc-alert-frame</code>, <code>--dc-alert-label-bg</code>, <code>--dc-alert-label-pad</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="callout-block">Callout block</h2>
<p>The multi-paragraph form of an alert. A variant word after <code>@callout</code> picks the register; <code>label</code> replaces the default label.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="160:169" class="language-markdown">@callout warning label=&quot;Heat is shared&quot;

**Heat is shared, not personal.** When any member of the crew draws Corporate attention, the whole crew's Heat track ticks up.

At Heat 3, expect a response. At Heat 5, the crew is actively hunted, and every public roll carries a complication.

@end-callout
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-note warning" data-callout="warning" data-label="Heat is shared" data-name="heat-is-shared" data-source-range="174:175"><span class="dc-alert-label">Heat is shared</span>
<p><strong>Heat is shared, not personal.</strong> When any member of the crew draws Corporate attention, the whole crew’s Heat track ticks up.</p>
<p>At Heat 3, expect a response. At Heat 5, the crew is actively hunted, and every public roll carries a complication.</p>
</div></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="184:185">
<tr data-source-range="184:185">
<th>Option</th>
<th>Values</th>
<th>Default</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="186:188">
<tr data-source-range="186:187">
<td>a variant word (<code>@callout vibe</code>)</td>
<td><code>note</code> <code>warning</code> <code>dm</code> <code>vibe</code> <code>origin</code> <code>location</code> <code>visit</code> <code>example</code></td>
<td><code>note</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="187:188">
<td><code>label</code></td>
<td>any text</td>
<td>the variant’s label</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 data-name="worked-example">Worked example</h2>
<p><code>@callout example</code> is the one spelling for a worked example: a play example, a sample exchange, a step-by-step. It is never a rule, and it reads that way: a cyan rail, a taped-on label that says <em>Example</em>, upright body text. Speech lines stay as <code>&gt;</code> lines inside it. (A bare <code>&gt;</code> blockquote is reserved for a flavor line.) A longer example can be a <code>@section</code>; this is for the short ones that sit inside a section.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="193:202" class="language-markdown">@callout example

The Dream Master: &quot;The guard turns toward you. What do you do?&quot;

You: &quot;I flip the noodle cart into the doorway.&quot; That is risky, so you **ROLL THE DIE!** and get a 14: a Success. The cart blocks the door.

@end-callout
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-example-callout" data-callout="example" data-source-range="207:208"><span class="dc-alert-label">Example</span>
<p>The Dream Master: “The guard turns toward you. What do you do?”</p>
<p>You: “I flip the noodle cart into the doorway.” That is risky, so you <strong><span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span></strong> and get a 14: a Success. The cart blocks the door.</p>
</div></div>
<p>A callout can also float beside the text: add <code>.dc-sidebar</code> (see <a href="#ch-layout" data-gp-source-token="[Layout](#ch-layout)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Layout</a>).</p>
<h2 data-name="dream-master-note">Dream Master note</h2>
<p><code>@dm-note</code> is shorthand for <code>@callout dm</code>, for the longer GM asides — scene setups, adjudication, design notes.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="223:232" class="language-markdown">@dm-note label=&quot;Running the Setup&quot;

The fixer offering this job doesn't know it's bait — they're a cut-out, paid to pass the contract along and ask no questions.

If the players investigate before accepting, they can smell the trap with a Streetwise roll, DC 14.

@end-dm-note
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-alert dc-dm-note" data-callout="dm" data-label="Running the Setup" data-name="running-the-setup" data-source-range="237:238"><span class="dc-alert-label">Running the Setup</span>
<p>The fixer offering this job doesn’t know it’s bait — they’re a cut-out, paid to pass the contract along and ask no questions.</p>
<p>If the players investigate before accepting, they can smell the trap with a Streetwise roll, DC 14.</p>
</div></div>
<h2 data-name="tape">Tape</h2>
<p>A strip of masking tape for a section break or a label between groups — tiers of NPCs, parts of a list: tan, torn at both ends, ink edges top and bottom, an ink label.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="252:255" class="language-markdown">&lt;div class=&quot;dc-tape&quot;&gt;Operators&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-tape">Operators</div>
</div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-tape-bg</code>, <code>--dc-tape-border</code>, <code>--dc-tape-color</code>, <code>--dc-tape-shape</code>. Add <code>dc-flush</code> (<code>&lt;div class=&quot;dc-tape dc-flush&quot;&gt;Label&lt;/div&gt;</code>) to run it edge to edge.</p>
<h2 data-name="rule">Rule</h2>
<p>A plain <code>---</code> between entries is a thin red rule — the separator for gear lists and NPC entries.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="270:277" class="language-markdown">Trauma Kit × 2

---

Signal Jammer (single-use)
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>Trauma Kit × 2</p>
<hr data-source-range="284:285">
<p>Signal Jammer (single-use)</p>
</div>
<h2 data-name="tags-and-class-tags">Tags and class tags</h2>
<p>Inline chips. <code>.dc-tag</code> is a keyword or cost label: a hard-edged chip with an ink hairline, in mono caps (tokens <code>--dc-tag-bg</code>, <code>--dc-tag-fg</code>, <code>--dc-tag-border</code>); <code>.dc-classtag.&lt;specialty&gt;</code> is a specialty identity with that specialty’s dot. These are raw HTML spans — the one place the system asks for it inline.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="294:299" class="language-markdown">&lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Melee&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Reach 2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-tag&quot;&gt;Loud&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class=&quot;dc-classtag augmerc&quot;&gt;Augmerc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;dc-classtag wirephreak&quot;&gt;Wirephreak&lt;/span&gt;
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p><span class="dc-tag">Melee</span> <span class="dc-tag">Reach 2</span> <span class="dc-tag">Loud</span></p>
<p><span class="dc-classtag augmerc">Augmerc</span>
<span class="dc-classtag proxy">Proxy</span>
<span class="dc-classtag streetwarden">Streetwarden</span>
<span class="dc-classtag gutterdruid">Gutterdruid</span>
<span class="dc-classtag cybersurgeon">Cybersurgeon</span>
<span class="dc-classtag wirephreak">Wirephreak</span>
<span class="dc-classtag technosorcerer">Technosorcerer</span>
<span class="dc-classtag etherlock">Etherlock</span>
<span class="dc-classtag dualist">Dualist</span>
<span class="dc-classtag generalist">Generalist</span></p>
</div>
<h2 data-name="roll-the-die">Roll the die</h2>
<p>The text <code>ROLL THE DIE!</code> in prose is picked out automatically, and so are <code>ROLL A DIE!</code>, <code>ROLLS A DIE!</code> and <code>ROLLING THE DIE!</code>: uppercase, with the exclamation mark, as written. It keeps the font and size of the text around it and is set bold, in a contrasting colour, and underlined. Inside a code span, raw HTML or a heading it is left alone.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="323:326" class="language-markdown">When an ability says ROLL THE DIE!, roll a d20 and read the outcome ladder. A trap may say ROLL A DIE! or ROLLING THE DIE! instead.
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<p>When an ability says <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span>, roll a d20 and read the outcome ladder. A trap may say <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL A DIE!</span> or <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLLING THE DIE!</span> instead.</p>
</div>
<p>Token: <code>--dc-accent-ink</code>, the text-safe ink of the component the phrase sits in (the deep magenta by default; a panel, a lede, a specialty’s cards and the like each set their own, so the phrase takes the colour of the box around it).</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 06-panels-and-cards.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter dg-guide" id="ch-panels" data-ch="6" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-6" data-name="panels-cards-data" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 6</p>
<h1 data-name="panels-cards-data">Panels, Cards &amp; Data</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">The enclosures: the section variants and the kinds of text they hold, three block registers, definition and glossary forms, the numbered procedure, the outcome ladder and roll tables, choice cards, item entries (gear, augments, treasure, vehicles, drugs), fields, catalogs, factions, tables, and the stat blocks a Dream Master runs from.</p>
<h2 data-name="section">Section</h2>
<p>A <code>@section</code> groups what is inside it. Bare, it is core’s plain box: no look of its own beyond a sheet of paper under the text (no text is ever set straight on the wall); a word after it picks the look. Two are general and work anywhere, and four more say what kind of text they hold (<a href="#kinds-of-text" data-gp-source-token="[below](#kinds-of-text)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">below</a>). Sections split across pages by default; <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> keeps one whole. One accent knob, <code>--dc-section-accent</code>, colours a panel’s rail and title bar. For the named components (<code>@npc-stat</code>, <code>@gear</code>, <code>@outcome</code> and the rest), <a href="#ch-start" data-gp-source-token="[Pick a section](#ch-start)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Pick a section</a> maps what you are writing to the marker.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="15:36">@section panel

## Rules Panel

The card: a cream substrate, an accent rail, a clipped corner, a shadow, and the first \`##\` or \`###\` as a title bar.

@end-section

@section flat

The rail on a plain host, for text that should read as a continuation of the page.

@end-section

@section

A bare section: no look of its own. The text sits on a plain sheet of paper.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-tall">
<div class="section dc-section-panel" data-section="panel" data-name="rules-panel" data-source-range="41:42"><h2 data-name="rules-panel">Rules Panel</h2>
<p>The card: a cream substrate, an accent rail, a clipped corner, a shadow, and the first <code>##</code> or <code>###</code> as a title bar.</p>
</div><div class="section dc-section-flat" data-section="flat" data-source-range="49:50"><p>The rail on a plain host, for text that should read as a continuation of the page.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="dg-stage dg-on-paper">
<div class="section" data-source-range="59:60"><p>A bare section: no look of its own. The text sits on a plain sheet of paper.</p>
</div></div>
<p><code>panel</code> is the look for rules text with a heading; <code>.dc-avoid-split</code> on it keeps a short panel on one page. For a short note set off from the text, use <a href="#ch-text" data-gp-source-token="[\`@callout\`](#ch-text)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0"><code>@callout</code></a>. Tokens: <code>--dc-section-bg</code>, <code>--dc-section-accent</code>, <code>--dc-section-pad-top</code>, <code>-right</code>, <code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code>, and for <code>panel</code> also <code>--dc-section-surface</code> and <code>--dc-section-shadow</code> (<code>none</code> removes the layer).</p>
<p>The components below that are sections have a marker of their own and a word on <code>@section</code>: <code>@npc-stat</code> (<code>npc</code>), <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> (<code>fiction</code>), <code>@citizen-walkthrough</code> (<code>walkthrough</code>), <code>@card-grid</code> (<code>cards</code>), <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> and <code>@dreams</code>.</p>
<h2 id="kinds-of-text" data-name="kinds-of-text">Kinds of text</h2>
<p><code>reference</code>, <code>lore</code>, <code>dm</code> and <code>choose</code> are panels that say what kind of text they hold, so a reader can tell a look-up from a rule from the world from advice for the Dream Master at a glance, in any chapter, in colour and in grayscale. Each cuts its own corners, as the skill cards do per specialty (a panel one larger chamfer, a reference four small even ones, lore long asymmetric slashes, dm a file-folder tab, choose ticket notches), and each has its own bar and sheet: a panel’s bar is solid accent on cream, a reference’s is a pale band, lore is deep violet, dm is ink with a cyan rule, and choose has no bar at all. The shape, the bar and the sheet say which kind it is before the colour does. Each has a word on <code>@section</code> and nothing else to learn: write the heading and the content as you would in a panel.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="75:76">
<tr data-source-range="75:76">
<th>You are writing</th>
<th>Write</th>
<th>Looks like</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="77:81">
<tr data-source-range="77:78">
<td>A table or list to look up: prices, conditions, a menu of options</td>
<td><code>@section reference</code></td>
<td>HUD blue, a pale title band ruled in ink above (a heavy inset rule) and below (a double rule) like a machined spec-sheet header, 10pt tight rows, four small even chamfers</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="78:79">
<td>The world, not the rules: places, factions’ history, setting</td>
<td><code>@section lore</code></td>
<td>A deep violet bar with a slashed top-left corner and a torn top edge over a pale lilac sheet, long asymmetric slashes (shallow at the top left, steep at the bottom right), italic sub-heads</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="79:80">
<td>Advice for the Dream Master, behind the screen</td>
<td><code>@section dm</code></td>
<td>A classified file folder: an ink tab in the box’s top band that carries the words <em>Dream Master</em> in cyan (it never splits from the box at a page break), an ink title bar with a cyan rule and a cyan rail over a cold concrete body, and one opposite corner cut (bottom right). The tab is drawn with or without a heading</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="80:81">
<td>A choice the player writes on the Citizen File</td>
<td><code>@section choose</code></td>
<td>A printed form, not a panel: a paper sheet a hair lighter than cream, sunk into the wall in an ink edge cut like a ticket (a V punched in the middle of each side, a perforated foot), no bar, an ink title over a double rule in the chapter’s accent, dotted write-in rules, heavy ink tick boxes and a yellow <em>Choose</em> stamp</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="82:122">@section reference

## Street Prices

| Item | Cost |
| --- | --- |
| Noodle voucher | 2 DC |
| Signal flare | 15 DC |

- Prices are for a Tuesday. Ask the fixer on a Friday.

@end-section

@section lore

## The Lower Monoverse

Where the city keeps what it would rather forget: dripping pipework, dead billboards, and neighbours who still wave.

@end-section

@section dm

## Running a Chase

Keep the pressure on. Let the dice say who gets away, and let the city complicate it.

@end-section

@section choose

## Pick a Quirk

- Hums when nervous.
- Never sits with their back to a door.
- Collects bus tickets.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage dg-tall">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-section-reference" data-section="reference" data-name="street-prices" data-source-range="127:128"><h2 data-name="street-prices">Street Prices</h2>
<table>
