<p><code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> and <code>@dreams</code> are <code>choose</code> sections that hold a run of <code>@card</code> (below); with no heading they draw nothing around the cards, which carry the look. The kind wins over the chapter: a <code>reference</code> panel stays HUD blue in a rust chapter, and <code>choose</code> takes the chapter’s accent only for its title rule and tick boxes. Tokens are the section’s own (<code>--dc-section-accent</code>, <code>--dc-section-bg</code>, and for the panel kinds <code>--dc-section-clip</code>, <code>--dc-section-rail</code>; a <code>choose</code> form has no cut corners or rail) plus the chip: <code>--dc-section-tab</code> (its text, <code>none</code> for no chip), <code>--dc-section-tab-bg</code> (yellow on <code>choose</code>) and <code>--dc-section-tab-fg</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="fiction-excerpt">Fiction excerpt</h2>
<p>Fiction, lore, a chapter-opening story. An aged page sunk into the wall, or revealed from behind it: a dark ragged hole torn in the wall with the page sitting back inside it, the hole casting a hard shadow down the page’s top and left and a light lip catching on its bottom and right, no rail, no title bar, narrative leading, and the first image floats left. On a chapter-start page the hole is deeper (a 10px shadow instead of 6px).</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="174:183">@fiction-excerpt

&quot;It's hard being me, but I guess it's the same for anyting sentient in the monoverse, ay?!&quot;

I wuz tearin down an alley, lungs burnin, heart jackhammering like it wanted out.

@end-fiction-excerpt
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-fiction-excerpt" data-source-range="188:189"><p>“It’s hard being me, but I guess it’s the same for anyting sentient in the monoverse, ay?!”</p>
<p>I wuz tearin down an alley, lungs burnin, heart jackhammering like it wanted out.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-fiction-bg</code> (the page), <code>--dc-fiction-line-height</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-depth</code> (the cast shadow’s depth, 6px) and <code>--dc-fiction-depth-bg</code> (its colour), <code>--dc-fiction-cavity</code> (the hole’s rim), <code>--dc-fiction-lip</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-rim</code> (how far the hole reaches past the page), <code>--dc-fiction-shape</code> (the page) and <code>--dc-fiction-hole</code> (the torn cavity), <code>--dc-fiction-rule</code> (an ink edge weight on the page, 0), <code>--dc-fiction-shadow</code> (an extra box-shadow on the page), <code>--dc-fiction-art-float</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-art-width</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="block">Block</h2>
<p>One macro, three registers. The class on the opener picks the silhouette and surface; <code>label</code> fills the title band and can be omitted.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="204:214" class="language-markdown">@block .dc-panel label=&quot;Action Economy&quot;

On your turn you get **one Move and one Action**. Spend Augment Points to push past it.

- **Move:** shift one Distance band. Free.
- **Action:** attack, hack, or trigger an ability.

@end-block
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-block dc-panel" data-label="Action Economy" data-name="action-economy" data-source-range="219:220"><div class="dc-block-title">Action Economy</div>
<p>On your turn you get <strong>one Move and one Action</strong>. Spend Augment Points to push past it.</p>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="223:224"><strong>Move:</strong> shift one Distance band. Free.</li>
<li data-source-range="224:226"><strong>Action:</strong> attack, hack, or trigger an ability.</li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="230:231">
<tr data-source-range="230:231">
<th>Variant</th>
<th>Register</th>
<th>Use for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="232:235">
<tr data-source-range="232:233">
<td><code>.dc-panel</code></td>
<td>HUD blue band, symmetric hex chamfers</td>
<td>Player-facing rules summaries, structured data</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="233:234">
<td><code>.dc-slate</code></td>
<td>A sign plate: octagonal corners in a heavy crimson frame, ink body, a blood band with a crimson rule and a stamped (double-edged) <em>Binding</em> tag</td>
<td>Binding rulings, Dream Master directives</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="234:235">
<td><code>.dc-shard</code></td>
<td>Light zine paper, a torn foot and a dog-eared corner, a taupe label strip, no accent hue</td>
<td>Fiction, atmosphere, setting asides</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The fourth register, <code>.dc-codex</code> (look-ups and glossaries), is retired: write a look-up as <a href="#kinds-of-text" data-gp-source-token="[\`@section reference\`](#kinds-of-text)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0"><code>@section reference</code></a>, a small box inside a section as <code>@block panel</code>, and a glossary as an <code>@definition</code> run.</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-block dc-slate" data-label="The Core Loop" data-name="the-core-loop" data-source-range="240:241"><div class="dc-block-title">The Core Loop</div>
<ol>
<li data-source-range="242:243">The <strong>Dream Master</strong> presents a situation.</li>
<li data-source-range="243:244">The <strong>Dreamers</strong> declare intent.</li>
<li data-source-range="244:246">The <strong>fiction resolves</strong> — sometimes on a die, sometimes on a word.</li>
</ol>
</div><div class="dc-block dc-shard" data-label="The Neon Bazaar" data-name="the-neon-bazaar" data-source-range="248:249"><div class="dc-block-title">The Neon Bazaar</div>
<p>Hologram haze cuts the gloom you’re stumbling through. Flickering ads claw the air above blades of neon, and the scent of burnt oil rides over hot garbage.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-block-bg</code>, <code>--dc-block-fg</code>, <code>--dc-block-accent</code>, <code>--dc-block-title-bg</code>, <code>--dc-block-title-fg</code>, <code>--dc-block-title-rule</code> (a whole <code>border-bottom</code> under the band), <code>--dc-block-tag</code> (a quoted word stamped on the band) and <code>--dc-block-tag-edge</code> (its border).</p>
<h2 data-name="definition">Definition</h2>
<p>A short dictionary-style entry for a term: in-world vocabulary, an NPC type, an item category. It is a cream page with a heavy rule over it and a hairline under it, the term set first as a blue headword, and the body in italic. Stack several in an <code>@columns 2</code> run for a glossary of entries, or put a run of <code>#### Term</code> entries in one <code>@definition</code> and it reads as a glossary: each term is a display-caps headword in blue, with a rule between entries. A <code>---</code> inside is the same rule, and a bold lead (<code>**Term** — gloss</code>) needs nothing.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="263:270" class="language-markdown">@definition

**Tick / Tic:** A heartbeat. The space between deciding and doing. Used in ability text to mean *immediate*.

@end-definition
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="275:276"><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="277:278"><p><strong>Tick / Tic:</strong> A heartbeat. The space between deciding and doing. Used in ability text to mean <em>immediate</em>.</p>
</div><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="283:284"><p><strong>Shortly / Shorty:</strong> Roughly a scene. Long enough to cross a district, tend a wound, or shake a tail.</p>
</div><div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="289:290"></div>
<div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="291:292"><p><strong>Fixer:</strong> The person who slides into the booth with a dirty envelope and a job you’ll regret.</p>
</div><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-source-range="297:298"><p><strong>Sporo:</strong> A native of Dimm City wearing an animal form.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h3 data-name="a-glossary">A glossary</h3>
<pre><code data-source-range="309:326" class="language-markdown">@definition

#### Heat

The running tally of attention you've drawn.

#### Hard Choice

A roll of 6–10. The fiction advances, but it costs you.

#### Twitch

An ability you use outside your turn when its trigger happens.

@end-definition
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block" data-name="heat" data-source-range="331:332"><h4 data-name="heat">Heat</h4>
<p>The running tally of attention you’ve drawn.</p>
<h4 data-name="hard-choice">Hard Choice</h4>
<p>A roll of 6–10. The fiction advances, but it costs you.</p>
<h4 data-name="twitch">Twitch</h4>
<p>An ability you use outside your turn when its trigger happens.</p>
</div></div>
<p>A box with its own heading and a divider is the same thing:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="351:362" class="language-markdown">@definition

#### Dice Etiquette

---

Roll your dice in the open. If a die lands off the table, it doesn't count: reroll it.

@end-definition
</code></pre>
<h3 data-name="lucid-and-surreal">Lucid and Surreal</h3>
<p>The two states of a roll get a definition each, one word on the marker, and they are drawn as opposites so a reader can tell them apart across the table. <code>@definition lucid</code> is sharp, aligned and in control: the lightest sheet, four even machined chamfers, a blue frame with a cyan hairline inside it, upright type and a headword ruled off in blue. <code>@definition surreal</code> is reality pushing back: an inverted, violet-black sheet with light type, a skewed outline torn along the foot, two inks (magenta and violet) off-register on opposite inner edges and a leaning headword. They differ in value, edge and type, so they stay opposites in greyscale. The body text is never rotated: only the headword leans.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="367:384" class="language-markdown">@definition lucid

#### Lucid

Roll 2d20, take the higher. You're sharp, aligned, in control.

@end-definition

@definition surreal

#### Surreal

Roll 2d20, take the lower. Reality pushes back.

@end-definition
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="389:390"><div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block dc-definition-lucid" data-definition="lucid" data-name="lucid" data-source-range="391:392"><h4 data-name="lucid">Lucid</h4>
<p>Roll 2d20, take the higher. You’re sharp, aligned, in control.</p>
</div><div class="gp-column-break" aria-hidden="true" data-source-range="399:400"></div>
<div class="dc-prose-panel dc-definition-block dc-definition-surreal" data-definition="surreal" data-name="surreal" data-source-range="401:402"><h4 data-name="surreal">Surreal</h4>
<p>Roll 2d20, take the lower. Reality pushes back.</p>
</div></div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-definition-block-surface</code>, <code>--dc-definition-block-accent</code> (the rules), <code>--dc-definition-block-term</code> (the headword), <code>--dc-definition-block-accent-width</code> and <code>--dc-definition-block-rule-bottom</code> (the rules’ weights; 0 for lucid and surreal), <code>--dc-definition-block-shape</code> (the silhouette; none by default), <code>--dc-definition-block-fg</code>, <code>--dc-definition-block-style</code>, <code>--dc-definition-block-frame</code> (the hard shadow, or an inset frame) and <code>--dc-definition-block-pad</code>. For a plain term list, a markdown definition list (<code>Term</code> / <code>: meaning</code>) needs no macro — see <a href="#ch-writing" data-gp-source-token="[Writing a Page](#ch-writing)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Writing a Page</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="procedure">Procedure</h2>
<p>A numbered instruction plate for anything the table runs in order: a cream plate with an ink frame and a hard shadow, a solid HUD-blue number strip down the left, 10pt ink step text with bold leads, ruled dividers, and a staple holding the plate to the wall. It sits on its own paper, never on the wall. The list may break between steps; a step is never cut.</p>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-steps-accent</code> (the number strip), <code>--dc-steps-surface</code> (the plate).</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="421:431" class="language-markdown">@procedure

1. **The Dream Master describes the situation.** Where you are, what's wrong.
2. **A Dreamer declares an action.** Say what your character does.
3. **If the outcome is uncertain, ROLL THE DIE!**
4. **The result determines what happens next.**

@end-procedure
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<ol class="dc-steps">
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">01</span><span><strong>The Dream Master describes the situation.</strong> Where you are, what’s wrong.</span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">02</span><span><strong>A Dreamer declares an action.</strong> Say what your character does.</span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">03</span><span><strong>If the outcome is uncertain, <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span></strong></span></li>
  <li><span class="dc-step-no">04</span><span><strong>The result determines what happens next.</strong></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
<h2 data-name="outcome-ladder">Outcome ladder</h2>
<p>The d20 result table every roll resolves against. One row per line: <code>roll | name | text</code>. Rows are colored by position, top to bottom: crit, hit, mixed, miss, fail. <code>@outcome flush</code> runs it edge to edge.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="451:462" class="language-markdown">@outcome

20 | Triumph | Best-case outcome. You do it, and the moment breaks your way.
11–19 | Success | You do it. Clean.
6–10 | Hard Choice | You succeed, but it costs you.
2–5 | Failure | You don't get what you wanted.
1 | Catastrophe | It goes bad, and then worse.

@end-outcome
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-outcomes" data-source-range="467:468">  <div class="dc-outcomes-label">Outcomes</div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row crit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-crit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Triumph</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">20</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">Best-case outcome. You do it, and the moment breaks your way.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row hit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-hit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Success</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">11–19</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You do it. Clean.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row mixed">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-mixed"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hard Choice</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">6–10</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You succeed, but it costs you.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row miss">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-miss"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Failure</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">2–5</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You don’t get what you wanted.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row fail">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-fail"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Catastrophe</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">1</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It goes bad, and then worse.</span>
  </div>
</div></div>
<p>The chip column is a quarter of the ladder wide (never under 130px), so a long band name wraps on two or three lines instead of cramping; band names are 10pt semibold, centred.</p>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-outcomes-surface</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-border</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-label-bg</code>, <code>--dc-outcomes-label-color</code>, <code>--dc-outcome-key-color</code>, <code>--dc-outcome-name-color</code>, <code>--dc-outcome-key-col</code> (the chip column’s width).</p>
<h3 data-name="a-table-of-the-five-bands-is-the-ladder">A table of the five bands is the ladder</h3>
<p>Write the result table you would write anyway. A two-column pipe table whose first column holds exactly the five standard bands (<code>20</code>, <code>11-19</code>, <code>6-10</code>, <code>2-5</code>, <code>1</code>, in either order) is drawn as the same ladder, in a skill card or anywhere else. The headers do not decide it (<code>Roll | Result</code>, <code>Result | Effect</code>, or empty); the ladder shows them in its label bar, over the two columns, and an empty header reads “Outcomes”. A band is read however it is typed: <code>11-19</code>, <code>11 – 19</code>, <code>**20**</code>. Any other set of bands (six bands, a split at 10) is not a ladder, and stays a plain table: for those, use <code>@roll-table</code> below.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="487:496" class="language-markdown">| Roll | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 20 | The jammed door gives. Something comes with it. |
| 11–19 | The door opens. |
| 6–10 | It opens, loudly. |
| 2–5 | It holds. |
| 1 | The handle comes off in your hand. |
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-outcomes">
  <div class="dc-outcomes-label dc-outcomes-head"><span>Roll</span><span>Result</span></div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row crit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-crit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Crit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">20</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The jammed door gives. Something comes with it.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row hit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-hit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">11–19</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The door opens.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row mixed">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-mixed"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hard Choice</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">6–10</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It opens, loudly.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row miss">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-miss"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Miss</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">2–5</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">It holds.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row fail">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-fail"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Catastrophe</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">1</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">The handle comes off in your hand.</span>
  </div>
</div>
</div>
<h2 data-name="roll-table">Roll table</h2>
<p>A random table that is not the ladder: one die, equal-weight rows, no tier colours. Wrap a pipe table in <code>@roll-table</code>; <code>label</code> names it and <code>die</code> picks the die on the chip (<code>d20</code> when you write none). Put the range in the first column (<code>1-4</code>, <code>5-8</code>, …) and the result in the second.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="515:528" class="language-markdown">@roll-table label=&quot;Pocket Lint&quot; die=d20

| Roll | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 1-4 | A bent key that opens nothing nearby. |
| 5-8 | Half a noodle voucher, still valid. |
| 9-12 | A dead earbud with a name scratched in. |
| 13-16 | A folded map of a different district. |
| 17-20 | A battered signal flare. |

@end-roll-table
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-roll-table" data-label="Pocket Lint" data-die="d20" data-name="pocket-lint" data-source-range="533:534"><div class="dc-roll-table-caption"><span class="dc-roll-table-die">d20</span><span class="dc-roll-table-name">Pocket Lint</span></div>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="535:536">
<tr data-source-range="535:536">
<th>Roll</th>
<th>Result</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="537:542">
<tr data-source-range="537:538">
<td>1-4</td>
<td>A bent key that opens nothing nearby.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="538:539">
<td>5-8</td>
<td>Half a noodle voucher, still valid.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="539:540">
<td>9-12</td>
<td>A dead earbud with a name scratched in.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="540:541">
<td>13-16</td>
<td>A folded map of a different district.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="541:542">
<td>17-20</td>
<td>A battered signal flare.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-roll-table-accent</code> (frame, caption and range rule), <code>--dc-roll-table-surface</code>, <code>--dc-roll-table-head-bg</code>.</p>
<h2 data-name="card">Card</h2>
<p>A choice card: heading, body, and a closing quote that becomes the card’s footer. Wrap a run of cards in <code>@flaws</code>, <code>@ideals</code> or <code>@dreams</code> for that step of character creation: each kind has its own colour, silhouette and a one-word chip. A Flaw is blood with a cracked edge, an Ideal is HUD blue with a steady ticket-stub shape (a V punched out of each side), a Dream is violet with a rising edge and a perforated foot. The heading is the first <code>####</code>: core turns only a <code>####</code> into the card’s heading, so write <code>####</code>.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="555:570" class="language-markdown">@flaws

@card

#### Addictive Personality

You chase instant gratification without weighing the consequences. The city always has one more hit on offer.

&gt; &quot;Just one more, and then I'll quit.&quot;

@end-card

@end-flaws
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-section-choose dc-flaws" data-source-range="575:576"><div class="dc-card" data-name="addictive-personality" data-source-range="577:578"><div class="dc-card-heading">Addictive Personality</div>
<div class="dc-card-body">
<p>You chase instant gratification without weighing the consequences. The city always has one more hit on offer.</p>
<blockquote class="dc-card-footer">
<p data-source-range="583:584">“Just one more, and then I’ll quit.”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div></div></div>
<p>The same card under <code>@ideals</code> is an ideal: HUD blue, a steady tablet:</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-section-choose dc-ideals" data-source-range="595:596"><div class="dc-card" data-name="honor" data-source-range="597:598"><div class="dc-card-heading">Honor</div>
<div class="dc-card-body">
<p>You believe in a code, and upholding it is your duty no matter the cost.</p>
<blockquote class="dc-card-footer">
<p data-source-range="603:604">“I made a promise to help those in need, and I’ll keep it at all costs.”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div></div></div>
<h2 data-name="gear-and-its-kinds">Gear and its kinds</h2>
<p>An item entry is a card with a name and the item’s prose. It is one marker, <code>@gear</code>, and a bare word on the marker line picks the kind, so a book’s catalog can tell them apart at a glance: <code>@gear</code> alone for general gear, <code>@gear augment</code> for cybernetics and mods, <code>@gear treasure</code> for magic items and relics, <code>@gear vehicle</code>, and <code>@gear drug</code> for drugs, elixirs and other consumables. Each kind has its own silhouette, surface, badge and name colour, so a reader knows what an entry is before reading it, and in grayscale: gear is a hang tag (a paper card in a heavy ink frame, a rust rail, an eyelet), an augment is a spec chip (a pale plate, a stepped notch, a cyan rule, a teal name), treasure is a gem (violet, an eight-sided cut, a facet), a vehicle is an industrial plate (octagonal cut corners, a heavy ink frame, a dotted seam of rivets, a yellow badge) and a drug is a prescription label (a wobbling organic outline, lime, an <em>Rx · Drug</em> pill). The kind badge is added for you; then your markdown. The name is the first <code>####</code> heading (<code>###</code> works too). A blockquote straight under it is the tagline.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="615:665">@gear augment

#### Kestrel Optics

&gt; Sees what you would rather not.

Two lenses in the orbit, a thermal layer, and a tiny voice that names what it sees. The chrome is rated for rain, not for sentiment.

**SPECS:** See in the dark to Far range. +1 to spot rolls. Needs a Neurolink to run the overlay.

@end-gear

@gear treasure

#### Lantern of Second Thoughts

&gt; A brass lantern with a very honest flame.

Whoever carries it hears the doubts of anyone standing in its light.

- **Rewind:** Once per cycle, take back one thing you just said.
- **Fast Forward:** Skip a conversation that was going nowhere.

@end-gear

@gear vehicle

#### Skyskiff Courier

&gt; Two seats, one fuel cell, no questions.

A ducted-fan runabout built for rooftops and bad ideas.

**SPECS:** IL: 3. Durability: 10. Mod Slots: 2.

@end-gear

@gear drug

#### Slowglass

A pale syrup sold in thimbles. The world gets quiet and a little too clear.

**Effects:** Reroll one failed roll this scene.

**Side Effects:** The next cycle begins with a Tough Choice you did not ask for.

@end-gear
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-augment" data-gear="augment" data-name="kestrel-optics" data-source-range="670:671"><h4 data-name="kestrel-optics">Kestrel Optics</h4>
<blockquote>
<p data-source-range="674:675">Sees what you would rather not.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Two lenses in the orbit, a thermal layer, and a tiny voice that names what it sees. The chrome is rated for rain, not for sentiment.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-label="SPECS"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>See in the dark to Far range. +1 to spot rolls. Needs a Neurolink to run the overlay.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-treasure" data-gear="treasure" data-name="lantern-of-second-thoughts" data-source-range="682:683"><h4 data-name="lantern-of-second-thoughts">Lantern of Second Thoughts</h4>
<blockquote>
<p data-source-range="686:687">A brass lantern with a very honest flame.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Whoever carries it hears the doubts of anyone standing in its light.</p>
<ul>
<li data-source-range="690:691"><strong>Rewind:</strong> Once per cycle, take back one thing you just said.</li>
<li data-source-range="691:693"><strong>Fast Forward:</strong> Skip a conversation that was going nowhere.</li>
</ul>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-vehicle" data-gear="vehicle" data-name="skyskiff-courier" data-source-range="695:696"><h4 data-name="skyskiff-courier">Skyskiff Courier</h4>
<blockquote>
<p data-source-range="699:700">Two seats, one fuel cell, no questions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A ducted-fan runabout built for rooftops and bad ideas.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-label="SPECS"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>IL: 3. Durability: 10. Mod Slots: 2.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-drug" data-gear="drug" data-name="slowglass" data-source-range="707:708"><h4 data-name="slowglass">Slowglass</h4>
<p>A pale syrup sold in thimbles. The world gets quiet and a little too clear.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-effects" data-field="effects" data-label="Effects"><span class="dc-field-label">Effects</span>
<p>Reroll one failed roll this scene.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-side-effects" data-field="side-effects" data-label="Side Effects"><span class="dc-field-label">Side Effects</span>
<p>The next cycle begins with a Tough Choice you did not ask for.</p>
</div>
</div></div>
<p>A <code>@gear</code> after another <code>@gear</code> closes the first, so a run needs an <code>@end-gear</code> only after the last entry. An entry with no name, or with two, is reported as a problem, and a word that is not one of the four kinds is reported and read as general gear. General gear keeps its own shape: an italic tagline paragraph and a bold price line, picked out by their shape (the price line is stamped as a dashed price tag).</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="723:734">@gear

### Throwaway Blaster

*Ranged. Pistol. CQC Locked, Pack Fed.*

**Range** Reach to Near only. **Damage** 2 on a Hit, 4 on a Triumph. Disposable after the run; serial-clean and untraceable.

@end-gear
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-gear" data-name="throwaway-blaster" data-source-range="739:740"><h3 data-name="throwaway-blaster">Throwaway Blaster</h3>
<p><em>Ranged. Pistol. CQC Locked, Pack Fed.</em></p>
<p><strong>Range</strong> Reach to Near only. <strong>Damage</strong> 2 on a Hit, 4 on a Triumph. Disposable after the run; serial-clean and untraceable.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-entry-accent</code> (rail), <code>--dc-entry-ink</code> (name), <code>--dc-entry-surface</code>, <code>--dc-entry-rail</code>, <code>--dc-entry-rule</code>, <code>--dc-entry-kind</code> (the badge word, a quoted string or <code>none</code>), <code>--dc-entry-shape</code> (the silhouette), <code>--dc-entry-radius</code>, <code>--dc-entry-ornament</code> with <code>--dc-entry-orn-inset</code>, <code>-orn-bg</code>, <code>-orn-border</code>, <code>-orn-clip</code> and <code>-orn-z</code> (the kind’s solid-fill ornament layer: a treasure’s facet, a vehicle’s rivets), <code>--dc-entry-frame</code>, <code>--dc-entry-badge-bg</code>, <code>--dc-entry-badge-fg</code>, <code>--dc-entry-badge-shape</code>, <code>--dc-entry-badge-radius</code>.</p>
<h3 data-name="fields">Fields</h3>
<p>A field is a labelled line of an entry: its rules, its traits, what a drug does and costs, what a power needs. Write <code>@field</code> with a variant word, and the variant’s name is the label; <code>label=&quot;…&quot;</code> replaces it, as with <code>@callout</code>.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="757:758">
<tr data-source-range="757:758">
<th>Variant</th>
<th>Default label</th>
<th>Look</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="759:765">
<tr data-source-range="759:760">
<td><code>specs</code></td>
<td>SPECS</td>
<td>the boxed rules strip: dark, with a yellow rail</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="760:761">
<td><code>traits</code></td>
<td>Traits</td>
<td>a plain line under a chip</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="761:762">
<td><code>equipment</code></td>
<td>Equipment</td>
<td>the same, in grey</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="762:763">
<td><code>effects</code></td>
<td>Effects</td>
<td>a solid teal frame on a pale teal ground, a + on the chip: the benefit; sits over side effects</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="763:764">
<td><code>side-effects</code></td>
<td>Side Effects</td>
<td>a dashed blood frame on a pale blood ground, a – on the chip: the cost</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="764:765">
<td><code>requires</code></td>
<td>Requires</td>
<td>a grey requirement bar</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="766:779">@field specs

Rolls of 19-20 are a Triumph while the lenses are tuned.

@end-field

@field requires label=&quot;Instrument&quot;

A disposable injector kit.

@end-field
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-source-range="784:785"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>Rolls of 19-20 are a Triumph while the lenses are tuned.</p>
</div><div class="dc-field dc-field-requires" data-field="requires" data-label="Instrument" data-name="instrument" data-source-range="790:791"><span class="dc-field-label">Instrument</span>
<p>A disposable injector kit.</p>
</div></div>
<p>You rarely need to type it. Inside an entry card, an <code>@npc-stat</code> and a <code>@skill</code>, a paragraph that opens with one of the labels the books already use is the same field: <code>**SPECS:**</code>, <code>**Traits:**</code>, <code>**Equipment:**</code>, <code>**Effects:**</code>, <code>**Side Effects:**</code>, <code>**Requires:**</code>, <code>**Wirephreak Gear:**</code>, <code>**Cybersurgeon Instrument:**</code> (also <code>Instruments</code> and <code>Instrument(s)</code>) and <code>**TECHNOENHANCEMENT:**</code>, in any case, with the colon inside the bold. The label stays in your own words. Anywhere else the paragraph is a paragraph. A label on its own line is a bare chip over the paragraph that follows. The list of labels is one table in <code>plugin.js</code>.</p>
<h3 data-name="catalog">Catalog</h3>
<p>A run of entries goes in a <code>@section catalog</code>: a panel with a title bar and an intro, whose entries are ruled instead of boxed. The title bar is a pale ledger band with an <em>Inventory</em> chip, not an accent bar, and an entry never splits across a column or a page. Each entry takes a rule in its own accent colour over it, so there is no <code>---</code> between entries (one left in draws nothing). Put the entries in <code>@columns 2</code> under the intro, as for any column run.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="804:841">@section catalog

## Optics and Ears

A short intro for the run.

@columns 2

@gear augment

#### Kestrel Optics

Two lenses, a thermal layer and a quiet voice.

**SPECS:** See in the dark to Far range.

@gear augment

#### Pinhole Ears

Hears a coin drop two rooms away.

**SPECS:** +1 to listen rolls.

@gear augment

#### Sable Voicebox

A voice that is never quite yours.

**SPECS:** Change your voice at will.

@end-columns

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-catalog" data-section="catalog" data-name="optics-and-ears" data-source-range="846:847"><h2 data-name="optics-and-ears">Optics and Ears</h2>
<p>A short intro for the run.</p>
<div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-source-range="852:853"><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-augment" data-gear="augment" data-name="kestrel-optics-2" data-source-range="854:855"><h4 data-name="kestrel-optics-2">Kestrel Optics</h4>
<p>Two lenses, a thermal layer and a quiet voice.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-label="SPECS"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>See in the dark to Far range.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-augment" data-gear="augment" data-name="pinhole-ears" data-source-range="862:863"><h4 data-name="pinhole-ears">Pinhole Ears</h4>
<p>Hears a coin drop two rooms away.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-label="SPECS"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>+1 to listen rolls.</p>
</div>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-entry dc-augment" data-gear="augment" data-name="sable-voicebox" data-source-range="870:871"><h4 data-name="sable-voicebox">Sable Voicebox</h4>
<p>A voice that is never quite yours.</p>
<div class="dc-field dc-field-specs" data-field="specs" data-label="SPECS"><span class="dc-field-label">SPECS</span>
<p>Change your voice at will.</p>
</div>
</div></div></div></div>
<h2 data-name="faction">Faction</h2>
<p>An organization’s profile: a card with a kind badge. The kind is the bare word: <code>megacorp</code>, <code>cabal</code>, <code>cult</code> or <code>enforcer</code>, and each has its own silhouette, top band, name colour and badge: megacorp a machined blue rectangle (an L-shaped step out of two corners) with a cyan edge, cabal violet with long asymmetric slashes, cult blood and torn along the top and foot with a broken rail, enforcer a sign plate (an octagon in a heavy frame) with a hazard-tape band and a yellow name label. <code>label=&quot;…&quot;</code> names a badge of your own; with no word there is no badge. Write the name as a <code>####</code> heading, then the fields and prose as you would anyway.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="888:907">@faction megacorp

#### Halcyon Reach Holdings

**HQ:** Tech District. **Led by:** Orla Vantine.

They own the lights and the water and would like to own the rest.

@end-faction

@faction cult

#### The Church of Static

Sing the signal and the signal sings back.

@end-faction
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-card dc-faction dc-faction-megacorp" data-faction="megacorp" data-name="halcyon-reach-holdings" data-source-range="912:913"><span class="dc-faction-kind">Megacorp</span>
<h4 data-name="halcyon-reach-holdings">Halcyon Reach Holdings</h4>
<p><strong>HQ:</strong> Tech District. <strong>Led by:</strong> Orla Vantine.</p>
<p>They own the lights and the water and would like to own the rest.</p>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-faction dc-faction-cabal" data-faction="cabal" data-name="the-quiet-table" data-source-range="922:923"><span class="dc-faction-kind">Cabal</span>
<h4 data-name="the-quiet-table">The Quiet Table</h4>
<p>A back booth, a rotating guest list, and a very long memory.</p>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-faction dc-faction-cult" data-faction="cult" data-name="the-church-of-static" data-source-range="930:931"><span class="dc-faction-kind">Cult</span>
<h4 data-name="the-church-of-static">The Church of Static</h4>
<p>Sing the signal and the signal sings back.</p>
</div><div class="dc-card dc-faction dc-faction-enforcer" data-faction="enforcer" data-name="blue-line-wardens" data-source-range="938:939"><span class="dc-faction-kind">Enforcer</span>
<h4 data-name="blue-line-wardens">Blue Line Wardens</h4>
<p>They keep the peace, for a fee.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Tokens: <code>--dc-faction-accent</code> (the rail and the badge fill), <code>--dc-faction-badge-fg</code>, <code>--dc-faction-shape</code>, <code>--dc-faction-surface</code>, <code>--dc-faction-band</code> (a box-shadow list: solid inset bands, so it prints as vector ink), <code>--dc-faction-band-height</code>, <code>--dc-faction-pad-bottom</code>, <code>--dc-faction-badge-shape</code>, <code>--dc-faction-title</code> (the name colour) and <code>--dc-faction-title-bg</code> / <code>-title-pad</code> (a label behind the name); a kind class sets them.</p>
<h2 data-name="tables">Tables</h2>
<p>Pipe tables carry every lookup: augment slots, weapon lists, roll tables, option tables, NPC statlines. No class, no macro.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="954:961" class="language-markdown">| Augment | Slot | Cost | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| Reflex Booster | Legs | 1,200 | +2 to initiative rolls |
| Subdermal Plating | Torso | 1,800 | Reduce bludgeoning damage by 1 |
| Optic Splice | Head | 900 | Ignore darkness penalties |
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<table>
<thead data-source-range="966:967">
