<h2 data-name="outcomes-inside-a-card">Outcomes inside a card</h2>
<p>A <code>| Roll | Outcome |</code> table inside a skill card renders as that card’s outcome ladder.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="475:504">@specialty .augmerc

@learning-path

### Example Path

@skill

#### Spit Flame | AUG2.1

&gt; Breathe deep. Let it out.

1. **1 AP** *Torch:* Vent a cone of ignited bio-fuel at everything in Reach. ROLL THE DIE!

| Roll | Outcome |
|---|---|
| 20 | Quadruple damage. |
| 11–19 | Double damage. |
| 6–10 | Double damage, but the target counters. |
| 2–5 | You miss. The target counters Lucid. |
| 1 | You fall prone and lose your next turn. |

@end-skill

@end-learning-path

@end-specialty
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-specialty augmerc" data-source-range="509:510"><div class="dc-learning-path dc-path-block" data-name="example-path-4" data-path-ref="AUG1" data-source-range="511:512"><div class="dc-path-shell">
<h3 class="dc-spray"><span class="dc-path-sticker">AUG1</span><span class="dc-path-sep"> </span>Example Path</h3>
</div>
<div class="dc-skill-card" data-name="spit-flame-aug2-1" name="spit-flame">
  <div class="dc-card-tab">
    <h4 class="dc-tab-title">Spit Flame</h4>
    <span class="dc-tab-tier">AUG2.1</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-card-body">
    <div class="dc-card-inner">
<p class="dc-flavor">Breathe deep. Let it out.</p>
<div class="dc-ability">
  <span class="dc-ap">1 AP</span>
  <p class="dc-ability-text"><em>Torch:</em> Vent a cone of ignited bio-fuel at everything in Reach. <span class="dc-roll-the-die">ROLL THE DIE!</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dc-outcomes">
  <div class="dc-outcomes-label dc-outcomes-head"><span>Roll</span><span>Outcome</span></div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row crit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-crit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Crit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">20</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">Quadruple damage.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row hit">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-hit"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hit</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">11–19</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">Double damage.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row mixed">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-mixed"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Hard Choice</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">6–10</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">Double damage, but the target counters.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row miss">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-miss"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Miss</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">2–5</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You miss. The target counters Lucid.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-outcome-row fail">
    <span class="dc-outcome-key tier-fail"><span class="dc-outcome-name">Catastrophe</span><span class="dc-outcome-roll">1</span></span>
    <span class="dc-outcome-text">You fall prone and lose your next turn.</span>
  </div>
</div>
</div></div></div>
</div></div></div>
<h2 class="gp-page-break" data-name="one-source-eight-shapes">One source, eight shapes</h2>
<p>The same path and card markdown, dropped into a different <code>@specialty</code>, takes that family’s silhouette and color with no change to the source. Here it is under Gutterdruid. (The Dualist and Generalist borrow other specialties’ skills, so they have no trees of their own.)</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="dc-specialty gutterdruid" data-source-range="546:547"><div class="dc-learning-path dc-path-block" data-name="beastmode" data-path-ref="GDR1" data-source-range="548:549"><div class="dc-path-shell">
<h3 class="dc-spray"><span class="dc-path-sticker">GDR1</span><span class="dc-path-sep"> </span>Beastmode</h3>
<div class="dc-intro">Some adapt and overcome. You mutate and dominate.</div>
<div class="dc-stickers"><span class="dc-sticker"><span class="dc-sticker-ref">1</span>Feralmorph</span><span class="dc-arrow">»</span>⁠<span class="dc-sticker"><span class="dc-sticker-ref">2</span>Environmental Assimilation</span></div>
</div>
<div class="dc-skill-card" data-name="feralmorph" name="feralmorph">
  <div class="dc-card-tab">
    <h4 class="dc-tab-title">Feralmorph</h4>
    <span class="dc-tab-tier">GDR1.1</span>
  </div>
  <div class="dc-card-body">
    <div class="dc-card-inner">
<p class="dc-flavor">You drop into a shape that hides in cracks or hunts the street.</p>
<div class="dc-ability">
  <span class="dc-ap">2 AP</span>
  <p class="dc-ability-text"><em>Take the Shape:</em> Your body rewrites into a natural form, from a mouse to a horse. The form has 6 HP; you gain its movement and senses but cannot use abilities, items, or language.</p>
</div>
<div class="dc-sub-header">Some problems need teeth, paws, and claws.</div>
</div></div></div>
</div></div></div>
<p>Skill-card tokens: <code>--dc-card-accent</code>, <code>--dc-card-surface</code>, <code>--dc-card-tab-bg</code>, <code>--dc-card-tab-title-color</code>, <code>--dc-card-gap</code>, <code>--dc-card-tab-shadow</code>; tier pill: <code>--dc-tier-bg</code>, <code>--dc-tier-fg</code>, <code>--dc-tier-edge</code> (the specialty’s own chip: its signal colour, or cream where that colour fails contrast for the type); AP chip: <code>--dc-ap-bg</code>, <code>--dc-ap-fg</code>, <code>--dc-ap-border</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 08-page-templates.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter templates dg-guide" id="ch-templates" data-ch="8" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-8" data-name="page-templates" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 8</p>
<h1 data-name="page-templates">Page Templates</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">A word on <code>@page</code> chooses a page’s geometry — margins, footers, running headers — and <code>@section</code> variants build the recurring pages of a Dimm City book. Each template below is a skeleton to copy; the finished page is in Part 2.</p>
<h2 data-name="page-geometry">Page geometry</h2>
<p>US Letter, perfect-bound, with bleed: the gutterpress <code>dtrpg</code> preset, declared in <code>manifest.yaml</code>. The geometry follows DriveThruRPG’s published print specification (<a href="https://help.drivethrupartners.com/hc/en-us/articles/12780800178583-Quick-Specifications-for-Print-Books" data-gp-source-token="[Quick Specifications for Print Books](https://help.drivethrupartners.com/hc/en-us/articles/12780800178583-Quick-Specifications-for-Print-Books)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Quick Specifications for Print Books</a>): a 0.125 in bleed on the three outside edges and none on the binding edge; all text at least 0.5 in inside the trim; art that does not bleed at least 0.25 in from the outside edges and 0.5 in from the binding edge.</p>
<p>The <code>@page</code> rules in the package’s <code>styles/page-rules.css</code> set the margins on the sheet, which includes the bleed, so each bleed-side margin is the trim distance plus 0.125 in.</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="17:18">
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>On the sheet</th>
<th>From the trim</th>
<th>DriveThruRPG minimum for text</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="19:26">
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td>Sheet (trim + bleed)</td>
<td>8.625 × 11.25 in</td>
<td>—</td>
<td>—</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td>Trim</td>
<td>—</td>
<td>8.5 × 11 in</td>
<td>—</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="21:22">
<td>Bleed</td>
<td>0.125 in at top, bottom and the outside edge; none at the spine</td>
<td>—</td>
<td>—</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="22:23">
<td>Top margin</td>
<td>0.625 in</td>
<td>0.5 in</td>
<td>0.5 in</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="23:24">
<td>Bottom margin (the folio and chapter chips sit in it)</td>
<td>0.875 in</td>
<td>0.75 in</td>
<td>0.5 in — the chips sit at the top of the band and end 0.5 in from the trim</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="24:25">
<td>Outside margin</td>
<td>0.625 in</td>
<td>0.5 in</td>
<td>0.5 in</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="25:26">
<td>Binding margin</td>
<td>0.75 in</td>
<td>0.75 in</td>
<td>0.5 in</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A page carrying the “Citizen File” running head has a 0.875 in top margin, so the head itself sits 0.5 in inside the trim. Margins swap per recto and verso: the binding margin is on the spine side. Extend any full-bleed image 0.125 in past the trim.</p>
<h2 data-name="page-words">Page words</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="31:32">
<tr data-source-range="31:32">
<th>You write</th>
<th>Footers</th>
<th>Use for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="33:42">
<tr data-source-range="33:34">
<td><code>@page</code></td>
<td><code>p.N</code> and <code>c.N</code> in opposing corners, the chapter’s name between</td>
<td>Every body page</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="34:35">
<td><code>@page contents</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>The contents page</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="35:36">
<td><code>@page credits</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>The credits page</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="36:37">
<td><code>@page intro</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>The introduction (a <code>@lede</code>, then a <code>@section flat</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="37:38">
<td><code>@page chapter-start</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>A chapter opener</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="38:39">
<td><code>@page citizen-file</code></td>
<td><code>p.N</code>, <code>c.N</code></td>
<td>Running header “Citizen File”</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="39:40">
<td><code>@page specialty-catalog</code></td>
<td><code>p.N</code>, <code>c.N</code></td>
<td>The specialty catalog: a two-page spread that opens on a left page</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="40:41">
<td><code>@page full-bleed</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>Zero margins; full-bleed art (core’s word; the raw <code>.dc-full-page</code> is styled the same)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="41:42">
<td><code>@page blank</code></td>
<td>none</td>
<td>A deliberately blank page (core’s word)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The <code>c.N</code> footer is the chapter number from <code>@chapter … ch=&quot;N&quot;</code>. A chapter split across files repeats its <code>@chapter</code> line in each.</p>
<h3 data-name="the-page-chips">The page chips</h3>
<p>The folio and the chapter number print as stickers, never as bare type. The folio (<code>P.121</code>) is a paper-cream sticker with an ink edge and a hard shadow; the chapter chip (<code>C.N</code>, which a book can restate as <code>E.N</code> as this guide does for Part 2) is a stamped coupon: a solid face with cream type and the same edge and shadow. Both are 17 pt tall with 10 pt type centred in the face, and both sit 0.125 in below the text block, inside DriveThruRPG’s 0.5 in text minimum, so a box that runs to the foot never touches them, on recto and verso, for every page word that prints them. The coupon is magenta by default; to colour it with a chapter, put the chapter’s pages on a named page, <code>:where(#ch-rules) &gt; .page { page: dc-chip-rust; }</code> (blue, rust, violet, amethyst or amber; the <code>specialty</code>, <code>rules</code> and <code>lore</code> chapter words do it for you). Name the page with the low-specificity <code>:where()</code> so a page’s own template name still wins. A <code>citizen-file</code> page’s running head is the same sticker in amber at the top.</p>
<h3 data-name="where-pages-break">Where pages break</h3>
<p>Pagination is part of the package, so every book gets it: text keeps three orphans and three widows in panels and callouts; a skill card splits between ability rows, never leaving one row on a page with a hand’s breadth of empty cream under it; headings, tape dividers and stat-block names stay with what follows. A panel splits where the page ends; one that must be kept whole whatever it costs is <code>{.dc-avoid-split}</code>, and a book that wants all its short panels whole says so from its own sheet (<code>--dc-section-break-inside: avoid</code>), since what counts as short depends on its content.</p>
<h3 data-name="text-on-the-wall">Text on the wall</h3>
<p>Nothing is ever printed straight on the brick. Anything you write directly on a page with no <code>@section</code> or callout around it dresses itself:</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="57:58">
<tr data-source-range="57:58">
<th>You write</th>
<th>It prints as</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="59:64">
<tr data-source-range="59:60">
<td><code># Title</code></td>
<td>A banner in the chapter’s accent colour with cream type</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="60:61">
<td><code>## Heading</code></td>
<td>A paper slip: an accent rail on the left, a dashed ink foot, a hard shadow and a strip of masking tape</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="61:62">
<td><code>### Heading</code></td>
<td>A tag that shrinks to its words, hung in from the left (every second one hangs further), no tape</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="62:63">
<td><code>#### Heading</code> and smaller</td>
<td>A label slip that shrinks to its words</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="63:64">
<td>Paragraphs, lists, quotes</td>
<td>One paper sheet per run; the sheet ends where the run does</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Headings that already carry a look (<code>{.dc-chevron}</code>, <code>{.dc-spray}</code>) and everything inside a section keep their own. The chapter word recolours the rail and the banner. To change the slip or sheet for a book, set <code>--dc-page-slip-bg</code>, <code>--dc-page-slip-ink</code>, <code>--dc-page-slip-edge</code>, <code>--dc-page-slip-shadow</code>, <code>--dc-page-slip-tape</code>, <code>--dc-page-slip-gap</code> (the space above a slip), <code>--dc-page-slip-offset</code>, <code>--dc-page-sheet-pad</code> or <code>--dc-page-title-ink</code>. A plain heading that follows a box (a ladder, a stat grid, a table) takes <code>--dc-heading-gap</code> above it. Explainer pages like the one you are reading (<code>@page .dg-doc</code>) print plain on white and are not wall pages.</p>
<h2 data-name="where-the-content-sits">Where the content sits</h2>
<p>A location is the page template or the chapter a section sits in. It draws nothing: it sets tokens, and the components read them. That is how one <code>@section panel</code> looks right in an opener, in a rules chapter and in a lore chapter without a rule per book.</p>
<p>A <strong>chapter word</strong> sets the colour register of every panel in the chapter. It recolours a panel’s accent and nothing else: an alert, a faction, a choice card or a gear entry keeps its own identity in every chapter, so a warning is a hazard sign and a Dream Master note is an ink slab wherever they sit:</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="74:75">
<tr data-source-range="74:75">
<th>You write</th>
<th>Section accent</th>
<th>Use for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="76:80">
<tr data-source-range="76:77">
<td><code>@chapter citizen-file</code></td>
<td>magenta-deep</td>
<td>Who you dream to be</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="77:78">
<td><code>@chapter specialty</code></td>
<td>blue-dark</td>
<td>The specialty gateway</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="78:79">
<td><code>@chapter rules</code></td>
<td>rust</td>
<td>Rules chapters</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="79:80">
<td><code>@chapter lore</code></td>
<td>violet</td>
<td>Setting and history</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<pre><code data-source-range="81:88" class="language-markdown">@chapter rules #ch-rules ch=&quot;3&quot;

@page chapter-start

# Are You Lucid Yet?
</code></pre>
<p>Any other word after <code>@chapter</code> is the chapter’s label, as before. A book adjusts a location with the same selectors and tokens, in its own sheet, and never with a component’s background, shadow or pseudo-elements:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="91:95" class="language-css">.chapter.dc-chapter-rules { --dc-section-accent: var(--blood); }
.page.dc-page-chapter-start .dc-fiction-excerpt { --dc-fiction-depth: 12px; }
</code></pre>
<p>A panel’s edge has tokens of its own, <code>--dc-section-clip</code> (the top-right cut, 18px), <code>--dc-section-cut-tl</code>, <code>--dc-section-cut-br</code> and <code>--dc-section-cut-bl</code> (the other three corners, 0) and <code>--dc-section-rail</code> (the rail, 5px), so a template changes the silhouette without touching the panel’s layers.</p>
<p>The old raw spellings (<code>@page .page-chapter-start .dc-chapter-start</code>, <code>.page-toc</code>, <code>.page-credits .dc-credits</code>, <code>.page-intro</code>, <code>.dc-citizen-file-page</code>) are no longer styled. Write the word.</p>
<h2 data-name="contents-page">Contents page</h2>
<p><code>@toc</code> turns an ordered list into the contents rows; a <code>[link](#id)</code> on an item resolves to its page number at build time.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="104:123" class="language-markdown">@page contents

# Contents

@lede

Twelve chapters of dreams, dirt, and what bites back.

@end-lede

@toc

1. [Who Do You Dream to Be?](#ch-citizen) — Citizen file, vibe, origins.
2. [What Do You Dream of Doing?](#ch-specialties) — How abilities work.
3. [The Augmerc](#ch-augmerc) — Muscle for hire.

@end-toc
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered: <a href="#ch-example-front-matter" data-gp-source-token="[Front Matter](#ch-example-front-matter)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Front Matter</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="credits-page">Credits page</h2>
<p>A chevron title, a lede for thanks and dedications, and the colophon, a <code>@section flat</code> with its names in <code>@columns 2</code>: bold lead-ins become role labels. The title of the contents, credits and intro pages is a chevron banner because of the page word; write <code>{.dc-chevron}</code> only on a heading anywhere else.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="130:155">@page credits

# Credits

@lede

**Special Thanks:** everyone who broke the rules so we could fix them.

@end-lede

@section flat

@columns 2

**Designers:** TWard and ITLackey

**Artist:** Scott Georges

**Edition &amp; ISBN:** First Edition · ISBN pending.

@end-columns

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered: <a href="#ch-example-front-matter" data-gp-source-token="[Front Matter](#ch-example-front-matter)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Front Matter</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="chapter-opener">Chapter opener</h2>
<p>A chapter opens on <code>@page chapter-start</code> with its <code># Title</code> (a banner in the chapter’s accent). Its pages carry the folio and chapter chip like any other page, so an opener that runs onto a second page stays numbered. A fiction excerpt can open the chapter: <code>@fiction-excerpt</code> sets narrative typography and floats the first image in the flow.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="163:190">@chapter #ch-citizen ch=&quot;1&quot;

@page chapter-start

# Who Do You Dream to Be?

@fiction-excerpt

&quot;It's hard being me, but I guess it's the same for anyting sentient in the monoverse, ay?!&quot;

![Lil Thump](img/placeholder-lil-thump.png)

I wuz tearin down an alley, lungs burnin, heart jackhammering like it wanted out.

@end-fiction-excerpt

@page

@section panel

## Citizen File

Thump is a PC created for dreams in Dimm City by an actual dreamer.

@end-section
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered: <a href="#ch-example-chapter-opener" data-gp-source-token="[Chapter Opener](#ch-example-chapter-opener)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Chapter Opener</a>. Tokens for the excerpt: <code>--dc-fiction-bg</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-depth</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-depth-bg</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-cavity</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-art-float</code>, <code>--dc-fiction-art-width</code>. On a chapter-start page the hole is deeper (a 10px cast shadow) by itself; nothing to add.</p>
<p>The snippet picker’s <em>Chapter Start Page</em> inserts this opener.</p>
<h2 data-name="citizen-file-walkthrough">Citizen File walkthrough</h2>
<p><code>@citizen-walkthrough</code> is the chassis for a character-creation step: a <code>####</code> field name and the prose that walks through it. Put two short fields side by side in <code>@columns 2</code> inside it. Pair it with <code>@page citizen-file</code> for the running header.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="199:218" class="language-markdown">@page citizen-file

@citizen-walkthrough

@columns 2

#### What's Yr Handle?

Choose a name. Pull it from a book, a show, a half-remembered dream.

#### Designation

Let others know how to refer to you. She/her, he/him, they/them, or something else entirely.

@end-columns

@end-citizen-walkthrough
</code></pre>
<p class="dg-result">Result</p>
<div class="dg-stage">
<div class="section dc-section-panel dc-citizen-walkthrough" data-source-range="223:224"><div class="gp-columns gp-columns-2" data-columns="2" data-name="whats-yr-handle" data-source-range="225:226"><h4 data-name="whats-yr-handle">What’s Yr Handle?</h4>
<p>Choose a name. Pull it from a book, a show, a half-remembered dream.</p>
<h4 data-name="designation">Designation</h4>
<p>Let others know how to refer to you. She/her, he/him, they/them, or something else entirely.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h2 data-name="specialty-catalog-page">Specialty catalog page</h2>
<p><code>@page specialty-catalog</code> plus a <code>@card-grid</code> of specialty cards, each in its own <code>@specialty</code>. The word makes the catalog a designed two-page spread: it opens on a left page, so the two pages face each other (the word forces no blank page when the chapter opener before it is on the facing right page, as it is meant to be; an opener that lands on a left page makes the engine insert one, so keep the opener on a right page); the title, four cards and Dualist fill the left page and the other four cards and Generalist the right (list Dualist fifth and Generalist last; each spans both columns; a lede of more than a line or two leaves no room for Dualist), in aligned rows, each card a poster with its portrait on top, its name, its tagline and its pitch. Whatever follows starts the next page: write a plain <code>@page</code> after <code>@end-card-grid</code>. Keep the cards uniform — identical chrome is what makes the choices comparable. The same <code>@card-grid</code> on no such page (as in the Variant Gallery) is the dense card with a small floated thumbnail.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="246:281">@page specialty-catalog

## Choose Your Specialty {.dc-chevron}

@lede

Every dreamer's got a sharp edge — your specialty is where it starts.

@end-lede

@card-grid

@columns 2

@specialty .augmerc

@specialty-card #specialty-augmerc

### Augmerc

![Augmerc](img/specialty-augmerc-card.png)

&gt; Cybernetic Commando

Heavily armed and wired for war.

@end-specialty-card

@end-specialty

@end-columns

@end-card-grid
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered with all ten cards: <a href="#ch-example-specialty-overview" data-gp-source-token="[Specialty Overview](#ch-example-specialty-overview)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Specialty Overview</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="specialty-profile">Specialty profile</h2>
<p>Intro, art plate, then learning paths with their skill cards, all inside one <code>@specialty</code>. The art plate takes its own page.</p>
<pre><code class="dg-split language-markdown" data-source-range="288:326">@specialty .augmerc

@specialty-intro

## Augmerc

…

@end-specialty-intro

@specialty-art

![Augmerc](img/specialty-augmerc-art.png)

@end-specialty-art

@learning-path

### Biting Distance

&gt; If you can touch it, you can maul it.

- Punishing Counter
- Rage Hit

@skill

#### Punishing Counter

…

@end-skill

@end-learning-path

@end-specialty
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered: <a href="#ch-example-specialty-profile" data-gp-source-token="[Specialty Profile](#ch-example-specialty-profile)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Specialty Profile</a>.</p>
<h2 data-name="choice-page">Choice page</h2>
<p>Flaws, ideals, and dreams: a <code>@flaws</code> (or <code>@ideals</code>, <code>@dreams</code>) of <code>@card</code> blocks. The section class sets each card’s colour, silhouette and chip (Flaw: blood, a cracked edge; Ideal: HUD blue, a steady tablet; Dream: violet, a rising edge). Cards stack in one column; use a tape divider between runs rather than columns.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="333:352" class="language-markdown">@ideals

## 4. Ideal

Your **Ideal** is the belief you fall back on when things get loud.

@card

#### Honor

You believe in a code, and it's your duty to uphold it.

&gt; &quot;I made a promise to help those in need.&quot;

@end-card

@end-ideals
</code></pre>
<h2 data-name="running-headers-and-footers">Running headers and footers</h2>
<p>Body pages print <code>p.N</code> (the page number) and <code>c.N</code> (the chapter number) in opposing bottom corners, swapping on verso. Front matter, chapter starts, and full-bleed pages print none. <code>c.N</code> comes from <code>@chapter … ch=&quot;N&quot;</code>: write it at the top of every chapter file and nothing else is needed.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

exports[`rendered output 09-publishing.md matches its snapshot 1`] = `
"<div class="chapter cli dg-guide" id="ch-publishing" data-ch="9" data-source-range="0:1"><div class="page dg-doc chapter-9" data-name="publishing" data-source-range="2:3"><p class="dg-kicker">Chapter 9</p>
<h1 data-name="publishing">Publishing</h1>
<p class="dg-lede">Three commands cover the whole lifecycle: preview while you write, build for output, validate for print. The app and the CLI run the same engine.</p>
<h2 data-name="commands">Commands</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="13:14">
<tr data-source-range="13:14">
<th>Command</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="15:21">
<tr data-source-range="15:16">
<td><code>gutterpress preview &lt;book&gt;</code></td>
<td>Live preview with hot reload for markdown, CSS, and assets</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="16:17">
<td><code>gutterpress build &lt;book&gt; --format pdf</code></td>
<td>Render a PDF through Chromium’s print engine</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="17:18">
<td><code>gutterpress build &lt;book&gt; --format pdfx</code></td>
<td>Render a PDF/X for a print service</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="18:19">
<td><code>gutterpress build &lt;book&gt; --format html</code></td>
<td>A static site of the book for the web</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="19:20">
<td><code>gutterpress validate &lt;book&gt; --pdf &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
<td>Source checks plus validation of a built PDF</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="20:21">
<td><code>gutterpress lint &lt;book&gt;</code></td>
<td>Print-safety lint over the book’s stylesheets</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><code>build --format pdf</code> and <code>pdfx</code> lint and validate before and after rendering unless you pass <code>--skip-lint</code>, <code>--skip-pre-validate</code>, or <code>--skip-post-validate</code>.</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="24:37" class="language-sh"># Preview a book
gutterpress preview my-book

# Print-ready PDF
gutterpress build my-book --format pdf --out ./dist/my-book.pdf

# Print submission: PDF/X-1a with an ICC profile
gutterpress build my-book --format pdfx --pdfx-flavor x1a --icc CGATS21_CRPC1.icc --out ./dist/my-book.pdf

# Static site
gutterpress build my-book --format html --out ./dist/site
</code></pre>
<h2 data-name="drivethrurpg">DriveThruRPG</h2>
<p>The package’s page geometry follows DriveThruRPG’s print specification: 0.125 in bleed on the three outside edges, all text 0.5 in inside the trim (<a href="#ch-templates" data-gp-source-token="[Page Templates](#ch-templates)" data-gp-source-occurrence="0">Page Templates</a>). With gutterpress 0.11.15 or later, <code>preset: dtrpg</code> covers the rest:</p>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="42:43">
<tr data-source-range="42:43">
<th>Setting (preset default)</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="44:46">
<tr data-source-range="44:45">
<td><code>print: { signature: 4, reserveLastPage: true }</code></td>
<td>Pads the interior to DriveThruRPG’s 4-page signature and always ends on a completely blank page — the page reserved for printer information. The build log’s page count is the number to enter in the cover Template Generator.</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="45:46">
<td><code>pdfx.blackText: k-only</code></td>
<td>In a <code>--format pdfx</code> build, near-black text (the book’s ink included) prints on the black plate only, as DriveThruRPG asks for text at 24 pt and below. Black rules and panels keep their rich black.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Don’t set <code>print.signature</code> yourself, and don’t end the book with a blank page of your own: the preset does both. (<code>@page .dc-printer-page</code>, the package’s stand-in from before gutterpress 0.11.15, was removed in 1.1.2.)</p>
<h2 data-name="flags">Flags</h2>
<table>
<thead data-source-range="51:52">
<tr data-source-range="51:52">
<th>Flag</th>
<th>Commands</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody data-source-range="53:60">
<tr data-source-range="53:54">
<td><code>--port N</code></td>
<td>preview</td>
<td>Port to listen on (default 3579)</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="54:55">
<td><code>--open</code></td>
<td>preview</td>
<td>Open the browser</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="55:56">
<td><code>--format</code></td>
<td>build</td>
<td><code>html</code>, <code>pdf</code> (default), or <code>pdfx</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="56:57">
<td><code>--out</code></td>
<td>build</td>
<td>Output directory, or a <code>.pdf</code> path for <code>pdf</code> / <code>pdfx</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="57:58">
<td><code>--pdfx-flavor</code></td>
<td>build</td>
<td><code>x1a</code> or <code>x3</code>, with <code>--format pdfx</code></td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="58:59">
<td><code>--icc</code></td>
<td>build</td>
<td>ICC profile; falls back to the manifest’s <code>pdfx.icc</code>, then the embedded profile</td>
</tr>
<tr data-source-range="59:60">
<td><code>--category</code></td>
<td>validate</td>
<td><code>source</code>, <code>pdf</code>, <code>asset</code>, or <code>heuristic</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 data-name="keeping-the-package-current">Keeping the package current</h2>
<p>A book pins an exact package version and builds from the copy vendored under <code>plugins/npm/</code>. To see whether a newer version exists and move to it:</p>
<pre><code data-source-range="65:69" class="language-sh">gutterpress ext outdated my-book            # each pinned package against npm's latest
gutterpress ext update gp-dimm-city my-book # re-pin to the latest; the old copy is removed
</code></pre>
<p>Commit the manifest; <code>plugins/npm/</code> is in the book’s <code>.gitignore</code>, and a fresh clone downloads the pinned version on its first build or <code>gutterpress validate</code>. The Gutterpress app does the same under Project settings → Features.</p>
<h2 data-name="this-guide">This guide</h2>
<p>From the package repository, <code>npm run dev</code> previews this guide against the working copy, <code>npm run build</code> renders it to <code>.build/design-guide.pdf</code>, and <code>npm run build:html</code> builds the static site. The developer workflow is in the repository’s <code>docs/developing.md</code>.</p>
</div></div>"
`;

